var tafsirData_4 = {
  "1": "This is a universal call to all human beings, not just Muslims. Allah is addressing every person who has ever lived or will ever live. Being 'mindful' (taqwa) means to be conscious of Allah, to fear displeasing Him, and to act with awareness that He sees everything you do. This is the foundation of all righteous behavior. When you truly remember that Allah is watching, you naturally avoid wrongdoing and strive to do good.\n\nAllah reminds humanity of their common origin. All human beings—regardless of race, nationality, or social status—came from one person: Adam (peace be upon him). This is a powerful reminder that racism, tribalism, and feelings of superiority have no basis in Islam. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said in his farewell sermon: 'All mankind is from Adam, and Adam was created from dust. An Arab has no superiority over a non-Arab, nor does a non-Arab have any superiority over an Arab.' This shared origin should create humility and brotherhood among all people.\n\nFrom Adam, Allah created his wife Hawwa (Eve). This shows that men and women share the same essence and origin. Neither gender is superior to the other in their humanity. They are partners meant to complement each other. The word 'mate' (zawj) implies companionship, comfort, and completion—marriage is meant to bring peace and tranquility.\n\nFrom this original pair, Allah caused humanity to multiply and spread across the earth. Every single human being is connected through this lineage. This reminds us that harming another person is like harming your own extended family. The diversity of nations and peoples all trace back to this single origin.\n\nPeople often invoke Allah's name when making requests or agreements, saying things like 'I ask you by Allah' or 'For Allah's sake, help me.' This verse reminds us to honor the sanctity of such appeals. When someone asks you in Allah's name, take it seriously. More importantly, be conscious of Allah in all your dealings with others.\n\nMaintaining family relationships (silat al-rahm) is a major obligation in Islam. The Arabic word for 'womb' (rahm) is derived from Allah's name Ar-Rahman (The Most Merciful). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that Allah said: 'I am Ar-Rahman, and I created the womb (rahm) and derived its name from My name. Whoever maintains family ties, I will maintain connection with him, and whoever severs family ties, I will sever connection with him.' This includes parents, siblings, uncles, aunts, cousins, and all relatives—even if they are difficult to deal with.\n\nThis is both a warning and a comfort. Allah sees everything—your actions, your intentions, how you treat your relatives, how you deal with others. Nothing escapes His knowledge. For the righteous, this is reassuring because Allah will reward their good deeds. For those who wrong others, this is a warning that they will be held accountable.",
  "2": "An orphan in Islamic terminology is a child who has lost their father before reaching puberty. When orphans grow up and become mature adults capable of managing their affairs, their guardians must hand over their property completely and honestly. This was revealed because some guardians in pre-Islamic Arabia would keep orphans' wealth for themselves, leaving the orphans with nothing when they grew up. Islam came to protect the vulnerable.\n\nSome dishonest guardians would swap their own low-quality items (like sick animals or damaged goods) for the orphan's high-quality possessions (like healthy livestock or valuable property). This verse forbids such fraudulent exchanges. Even if the items appear similar on the surface, taking advantage of an orphan's inability to protect their interests is strictly forbidden.\n\nAnother deceptive practice was to mix the orphan's money or property with one's own, making it impossible to distinguish between them. Then the guardian would claim everything belonged to them. This verse prohibits any scheme designed to unlawfully consume an orphan's wealth.\n\nStealing from orphans is not a minor mistake—it is among the major sins in Islam. The Prophet (peace be upon him) listed 'consuming the wealth of orphans' among the seven destructive sins that a Muslim must avoid. Orphans have already lost their protector (their father); taking advantage of their vulnerability compounds the injustice.",
  "3": "This verse was revealed to address a specific problem. Some guardians would marry the orphan girls under their care to gain control of their wealth, but then mistreat them or refuse to give them their proper dowry. Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) explained that this verse addressed men who wanted to marry orphan women for their beauty or wealth but were unwilling to give them fair treatment.\n\nIf you cannot be just to orphan women, then leave them alone and marry other women instead. This verse permits polygamy (up to four wives) but frames it within the context of justice. The permission is not unlimited—it comes with strict conditions.\n\nJustice between wives includes equal time, equal financial support, and equal treatment in all material matters. If a man doubts his ability to be fair, he must limit himself to one wife. The Quran itself later states: 'You will never be able to be completely fair between wives, even if you try' (4:129), indicating that complete emotional equality is impossible, but material fairness is required.\n\nThis refers to the historical context where captives from war could become part of a household. Islam regulated this existing practice with rights and protections, and the overall direction of Islamic teachings encouraged freeing such individuals.\n\nThe purpose of these restrictions is to prevent oppression. Marriage should bring comfort and stability, not become a means of exploiting women. By limiting the number of wives and emphasizing justice, Islam protects women's rights.",
  "4": "The dowry (mahr) is the wife's absolute right. It is a gift from the husband to the wife, and it becomes her property alone. The husband has no right to take it back or control how she uses it. The word 'graciously' (nihlah) means it should be given willingly, happily, and without resentment—not as a burden but as an honor.\n\nIf a wife, of her own free choice and without pressure, decides to give back part or all of her dowry to her husband, he may accept it. The key word is 'willingly'—there must be no coercion, manipulation, or family pressure. This shows Islam's respect for women's autonomy over their own property.",
  "5": "Some people—whether due to mental incapacity, extreme youth, or poor judgment—are not able to manage money responsibly. Giving them direct control over significant wealth could lead to its loss, harming both them and others who depend on that wealth. This verse instructs guardians to manage such wealth responsibly rather than handing it over to those who would waste it.\n\nEven though they don't control the wealth directly, their needs must be fully met. They should not be deprived of food, clothing, shelter, or any necessities. The wealth should serve them even if they don't manage it themselves.\n\nPeople who cannot manage their own affairs often feel frustrated, embarrassed, or inferior. Islam commands that they be treated with dignity and kindness. Harsh words, mockery, or condescension are forbidden. Their human dignity must be preserved even when their financial independence is limited.",
  "6": "Before handing over an orphan's wealth, the guardian must verify that the orphan is mature enough to handle it responsibly. This testing involves observing their decision-making abilities, giving them small amounts to manage, and seeing how they handle financial matters. The 'marriageable age' refers to physical and mental maturity—when they can understand contracts, marriage, and financial responsibilities.\n\nMaturity alone is not enough; the orphan must also demonstrate rushd—sound judgment and the ability to manage money wisely. If a young person reaches adulthood but still makes foolish financial decisions, the guardian may delay handing over the wealth until they develop better judgment. This protects the orphan from their own inexperience.\n\nSome guardians would rush to spend the orphan's wealth while they were still young, fearing that once the orphan grew up, they would demand an accounting. This verse forbids such behavior. The guardian must preserve the wealth, not deplete it.\n\nA wealthy guardian should serve the orphan without taking any payment from the orphan's wealth. This is an act of charity and righteousness that will be rewarded by Allah.\n\nIslam is practical. If a guardian is poor and spends their time and effort managing an orphan's affairs, they may take a fair and modest amount as compensation for their work—not as profit, but as reasonable payment for their labor.\n\nTo protect both the guardian and the orphan from future disputes, the handover should be documented with witnesses. This prevents the orphan from later claiming they received less than they were owed, and it protects the guardian from false accusations.\n\nEven if no human witnesses are present, Allah sees everything. He keeps perfect accounts of every transaction. This is a reminder that even if someone cheats an orphan and gets away with it in this world, Allah will hold them accountable on the Day of Judgment.",
  "7": "Islam establishes that men have a God-given right to inherit from their deceased relatives. This was significant because some cultures excluded certain family members from inheritance entirely.\n\nThis was revolutionary. In pre-Islamic Arabia, women often received nothing from inheritance—sometimes they themselves were inherited as property! Islam firmly established women's right to inherit, making it a divine law that cannot be denied.\n\nThe inheritance laws apply regardless of the size of the estate. Whether the deceased left behind vast wealth or modest possessions, the shares must be distributed according to Allah's law.\n\nThese are not suggestions or recommendations—they are mandatory. No one has the right to deprive an heir of their share or to alter Allah's prescribed distribution.",
  "8": "When the inheritance is being divided, there may be people present who are not entitled to a legal share—perhaps distant relatives, orphans from the community, or poor people.\n\nOut of kindness and generosity, give them something from the estate before the formal distribution. This is an act of charity that brings blessings and maintains social bonds.\n\nIf you cannot give them anything (perhaps because the estate is small), at least speak to them with kindness and respect. Don't make them feel embarrassed for being present or for their need.",
  "9": "This verse appeals to human empathy. Imagine if you died and left behind young children—how would you want others to treat them? You would want people to be kind, honest, and protective of your children's rights and property. Treat orphans under your care with the same concern and compassion you would want for your own children if they became orphans.\n\nActing justly toward orphans is ultimately about fearing Allah. Human supervision may fail, but Allah's supervision never does.\n\nEven the words spoken to orphans matter. Guardians should not speak harshly, mockingly, or dismissively to orphans. They deserve kind, respectful, and fair speech that preserves their dignity.",
  "10": "This refers to anyone who takes what belongs to an orphan without right—whether through theft, fraud, or mismanagement.\n\nThis is a vivid and terrifying image. What they eat from the orphan's wealth is not nourishment—it is fire that will burn them from within. The Quran uses this imagery to show that ill-gotten wealth brings no benefit; it only brings destruction.\n\nThe punishment continues in the Hereafter. Those who wronged orphans will face the Fire of Hell. This severe warning shows how seriously Allah takes the protection of vulnerable members of society.",
  "11": "This is Allah's command, not a human decision that can be debated. The male receives double the female's share in this specific situation. This is because Islamic law requires men to financially support their wives, children, and other dependents, while women's earnings and inheritance are entirely their own. A woman keeps her inheritance for herself; a man must spend his on his family. When these responsibilities are considered, the system is balanced.\n\nWhen the deceased has only daughters (two or more) and no sons, the daughters together receive two-thirds of the estate.\n\nA single daughter receives half of the estate.\n\nIf the deceased has children, each of their parents (the deceased's father and mother) receives one-sixth of the estate.\n\nIf someone dies without children and only their parents inherit, the mother receives one-third and the father receives the remaining two-thirds.\n\nIf the deceased has siblings (even if they don't inherit), the mother's share is reduced to one-sixth because the father will need to support those siblings.\n\nBefore any inheritance is distributed, two things must be settled first: (1) any debts the deceased owed must be paid, and (2) any bequests (wasiyyah) the deceased made (up to one-third of the estate for non-heirs) must be fulfilled.\n\nYou might think your children will benefit you more than your parents, or vice versa. But only Allah knows the unseen. Perhaps your parents' prayers for you are more valuable than your children's help. Trust Allah's distribution.\n\nThese laws come from the One who knows everything and whose wisdom is perfect. Even if humans don't fully understand the reasoning, they should trust that Allah's system is the most just and beneficial.",
  "12": "A husband inherits half of his deceased wife's estate if she had no children.\n\nIf the wife had children (whether from this husband or a previous marriage), the husband's share is reduced to one-fourth.\n\nThe same principle applies—settle obligations first.\n\nA wife inherits one-fourth of her deceased husband's estate if he had no children.\n\nIf the husband had children, the wife's share is one-eighth. If there are multiple wives, they share this one-eighth among themselves.\n\nThis addresses the case of someone who dies with no parents and no children (called kalalah). If they have maternal half-siblings (siblings from the same mother but different fathers), each sibling gets one-sixth.\n\nIf there are multiple maternal half-siblings, they share one-third equally among themselves, regardless of gender.\n\nBequests and debts must not be used to harm the rightful heirs. For example, someone cannot make a bequest specifically designed to deprive their heirs of their shares or falsely claim debts.\n\nAllah knows all circumstances and is patient with human weaknesses, but His laws must be followed.",
  "13": "The inheritance shares are divine boundaries that must not be crossed. They are not suggestions but fixed laws.\n\nThose who follow these laws—respecting the rights of heirs and distributing wealth justly—will be rewarded with Paradise.\n\nThe reward is eternal, not temporary. This is the greatest success anyone can achieve.",
  "14": "Those who violate the inheritance laws—taking more than their share, depriving others of their rights, or manipulating the system—are transgressors.\n\nThe punishment for such disobedience, if one considers it permissible to violate Allah's laws, is eternal Hellfire.\n\nBeyond the physical torment, there will be shame and disgrace. Those who stole from the weak and vulnerable will be humiliated before all of creation.",
  "15": "This refers to sexual relations outside of marriage (zina), which is a major sin in Islam that destroys families, spreads disease, confuses lineage, and corrupts society.\n\nTo prove such a serious accusation, four trustworthy male witnesses must testify that they directly witnessed the act. This extremely high standard of proof protects people from false accusations and slander. In practice, this means the act must have been committed openly and brazenly.\n\nThis was an early ruling that was later replaced (abrogated) by the punishment specified in Surah An-Nur (24:2)—one hundred lashes for unmarried offenders. For married offenders, the punishment established by the Prophet's practice is stoning. The phrase 'or Allah ordains a different way' indicated that a more specific ruling would come later.",
  "16": "Both parties involved in the sin should face consequences. The 'discipline' mentioned here was later specified through subsequent revelations and prophetic guidance.\n\nIf the offenders sincerely repent and correct their behavior, they should not be continually punished or socially ostracized. Islam believes in rehabilitation and giving people the chance to change.\n\nAllah's mercy is vast. No matter how great the sin, sincere repentance can erase it. Allah loves to forgive those who turn back to Him.",
  "17": "'Ignorantly' here doesn't mean they didn't know it was wrong, but rather that they acted impulsively, driven by desire, without fully considering the consequences. Every sin involves a degree of 'ignorance' in the sense that the person ignores Allah's warning at the moment of sinning.\n\nThe repentance should come quickly—before death approaches. 'Soon' means not delaying repentance indefinitely, thinking one can sin now and repent later. The scholars say as long as the soul has not reached the throat (the moment of death), repentance is still possible.\n\nAllah knows who is sincere and who is merely pretending. His wisdom determines how He deals with each person's repentance.",
  "18": "Some people deliberately continue sinning, planning to repent at the last moment before death. But when death actually comes, their 'repentance' is worthless. Pharaoh tried this—when he was drowning, he suddenly declared faith, but it was too late. True repentance requires time to prove its sincerity through changed behavior.\n\nThose who reject faith entirely and die in that state cannot benefit from repentance. The opportunity for repentance ends at death.\n\nBoth groups—those who delay repentance until death and those who die as disbelievers—face severe punishment in the Hereafter.",
  "19": "In pre-Islamic Arabia, when a man died, his male relatives would sometimes 'inherit' his widow as part of his estate. They might marry her themselves without her consent or prevent her from remarrying so they could keep her dowry. Islam abolished this cruel practice entirely. Women are human beings with rights, not property to be inherited.\n\nSome men would treat their wives so badly that the women would give back their dowry just to escape the marriage (this is called khul'). Using cruelty as a tool to recover the dowry is absolutely forbidden.\n\nThe only exception is if the wife commits clear, proven adultery. In such a case, different rules may apply regarding the dowry.\n\nEven if you don't love your wife, you must still treat her with fairness and respect. Islam commands good treatment regardless of personal feelings.\n\nA marriage you find difficult might produce righteous children, bring unexpected blessings, or teach you patience. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'A believing man should not hate a believing woman. If he dislikes one of her characteristics, he will be pleased with another.' Don't make hasty decisions based on temporary feelings.",
  "20": "If a man decides to divorce one wife and marry another, he must still treat the first wife justly.\n\nEven if the dowry was enormous—a 'qintar' (a massive amount) of gold—it belongs to her.\n\nThe dowry, once given, is the wife's property forever. Divorce does not give the husband any right to reclaim it.\n\nThis rhetorical question emphasizes how wrong it would be. Taking back the dowry combines injustice (wronging another person) with sin (violating Allah's command).",
  "21": "Marriage involves the most personal and intimate relationship between two people. After sharing such closeness, how could a man justify taking back what he gave her?\n\nMarriage in Islam is described as a 'mithaq ghalith'—a solemn, firm covenant. It's not a casual arrangement but a sacred contract witnessed by Allah. This emphasizes the seriousness of marriage and the weight of the commitments made.",
  "22": "Marrying a woman who was married to your father is absolutely forbidden in Islam. This includes your biological father, and scholars extend it to grandfathers as well. Such a marriage would be considered incestuous and would violate the honor and structure of the family.\n\nThose who had such marriages before Islam are not punished for what happened in ignorance, but they must end such relationships after accepting Islam.\n\nThree words are used to condemn this practice: (1) 'shameful' (fahishah)—morally outrageous; (2) 'despicable' (maqt)—hateful and disgusting; (3) 'evil' (sa'a sabila)—a terrible path. This strong language leaves no doubt about how seriously Islam views this prohibition.",
  "23": "These are permanently forbidden due to direct blood relationships. 'Mothers' includes grandmothers, 'daughters' includes granddaughters, and 'sisters' includes both full sisters and half-sisters.\n\nSisters of your father and sisters of your mother, and their preceding generations, are all forbidden.\n\nNieces from both brothers and sisters are forbidden, including their descendants.\n\nWomen who breastfed you during infancy become like mothers to you. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'What becomes forbidden through breastfeeding is the same as what becomes forbidden through blood relationships.'\n\nGirls who were breastfed by the same woman who breastfed you are like sisters to you.\n\nThe moment you marry a woman, her mother becomes permanently forbidden to you, even if you later divorce her daughter.\n\nIf you marry a woman who has a daughter from a previous marriage, that daughter becomes forbidden to you—but only if you consummated the marriage with her mother. If you divorced the mother before consummation, the daughter is not forbidden.\n\nThe wives of your biological sons are permanently forbidden. This does not include adopted sons, as Islam does not recognize adoption in the sense of changing lineage.\n\nYou cannot be married to two sisters simultaneously. If one dies or you divorce her (and her waiting period ends), you can marry her sister.\n\nPrevious marriages that violated these rules before one knew the law are forgiven.\n\nAllah forgives past mistakes made in ignorance and shows mercy to those who repent and correct their ways.",
  "24": "A woman who is already married to someone else is forbidden. The exception refers to the historical context of captives from war whose previous marriages were dissolved by their captivity.\n\nThese prohibitions are divine law, not human customs that can be changed.\n\nAny woman not mentioned in the prohibited categories is potentially lawful for marriage.\n\nMarriage must be conducted properly—with a dowry, witnesses, and consent. Illicit relationships are forbidden.\n\nThe dowry becomes obligatory after consummation of the marriage.\n\nIf both parties agree to adjust the dowry—whether the wife forgives some of it or the husband gives more—that is permissible.\n\nAllah's laws are based on perfect knowledge and wisdom, designed for human benefit.",
  "25": "This verse addresses the historical reality of slavery and provides guidance for those who could not afford the dowry required to marry a free woman. Marrying a believing bondwoman was presented as an option, though marrying a free woman was preferable.\n\nDon't look down on bondwomen. Allah knows their true faith, which might be stronger than that of free women. External status doesn't determine spiritual worth.\n\nFree people and bondspeople are all human beings, all from Adam. There should be no arrogance based on social status.\n\nEven bondwomen have the right to a proper marriage contract and dowry. Their owners must consent to the marriage, and the dowry must be fair.\n\nThe bondwomen should be of good character—not engaging in open immorality or secret relationships.\n\nBecause bondwomen had less social protection and education, their punishment for certain offenses was half that of free women. This shows consideration for circumstances.\n\nThis permission to marry bondwomen is for those who genuinely struggle to remain chaste and cannot afford to marry free women.\n\nPatience—waiting until one can afford to marry a free woman—is described as better. The permission to marry bondwomen was a concession, not the ideal.\n\nAllah understands human weakness and provides lawful outlets while forgiving those who struggle.",
  "26": "Allah does not leave humanity in confusion. He explains His laws clearly so people know exactly what is permitted and forbidden.\n\nThe moral path Islam teaches is the same path followed by righteous people throughout history—prophets, saints, and the sincere believers of previous nations.\n\nAllah wants to forgive you and bring you back to Him. His intention is mercy, not punishment.\n\nAllah's guidance is based on complete knowledge of human nature and perfect wisdom about what benefits humanity.",
  "27": "Allah actively wants to show you favor, forgive your sins, and guide you to success.\n\nPeople who are slaves to their desires want you to join them in sin. Misery loves company. They try to normalize immorality and make following Allah's guidance seem restrictive or outdated.",
  "28": "Islam is meant to make life easier, not harder. The rules exist to protect people, not to burden them unnecessarily. When there is hardship, there is usually a concession.\n\nHumans are inherently weak—weak in resisting temptation, weak in patience, weak in physical endurance. Allah knows this and designs His laws accordingly. This verse should inspire humility, not excuse sin. We are weak, so we need Allah's help and guidance even more.",
  "29": "This comprehensive prohibition covers all forms of unlawful financial gain: theft, fraud, bribery, usury (interest), gambling, cheating in business, breaking contracts, and any other means of taking what doesn't belong to you.\n\nLawful trade requires both parties to agree willingly, with full knowledge of what they're getting. No deception, no coercion, no hidden defects. This principle establishes the foundation of ethical commerce.\n\nThis prohibition covers both murder and suicide. Killing another person unjustly is one of the greatest sins. Killing yourself is also forbidden—your life belongs to Allah, not to you. The scholars also extend this to anything that gradually destroys your health, like harmful substances.\n\nThese prohibitions come from Allah's mercy, not His desire to restrict you. They protect life, property, and society.",
  "30": "Whoever violates these commands—whether by taking others' wealth illegally or taking life—and does so deliberately with full knowledge that it is wrong...\n\nThe punishment is Hellfire. Allah does not make empty threats.\n\nExecuting this punishment is effortless for Allah. He is not limited in His power. Those who think they can escape accountability are deluding themselves.",
  "31": "Major sins (kaba'ir) include things like shirk (associating partners with Allah), murder, adultery, consuming interest (riba), stealing, false testimony, and other serious transgressions specifically warned against in the Quran and Sunnah.\n\nThis is a beautiful promise. If you stay away from the major sins, Allah will forgive your minor sins. Regular prayers, fasting, charity, and other good deeds also erase minor sins. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'The five daily prayers and Friday prayer to the next Friday prayer are expiation for whatever sins come in between, so long as one avoids major sins.'\n\nThe reward for avoiding major sins is not just forgiveness but an honorable entrance into Paradise. This 'place of honor' (mudkhal kareem) suggests dignity, respect, and a noble position in the Hereafter.",
  "32": "This verse addresses the natural human tendency toward envy and jealousy. Allah distributes His blessings differently among people—some have more wealth, others have better health, some have more children, others have greater knowledge. Instead of wishing you had what others have, accept what Allah has given you. Envy (hasad) is destructive; it burns the heart and leads to ingratitude. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Beware of envy, for envy consumes good deeds just as fire consumes wood.' This also applies to differences between men and women—each has been given different roles and responsibilities, and neither should wish to be the other.\n\nBoth men and women earn rewards based on their own actions. Allah judges people by their deeds, not their gender. A woman's good deeds are rewarded just as a man's are. This verse establishes complete spiritual equality before Allah while acknowledging that men and women may have different roles in society.\n\nInstead of envying others, turn to Allah and ask Him to bless you from His infinite treasures. Du'a (supplication) is the weapon of the believer. Allah's bounties are unlimited—when He gives to one person, it doesn't diminish what He can give to others. So ask Allah directly rather than coveting what He has given others.\n\nAllah knows exactly what each person needs and what is best for them. Sometimes what appears to be a blessing is actually a test, and what seems like deprivation is actually protection. Trust Allah's wisdom in His distribution.",
  "33": "Allah has established a system of inheritance that ensures wealth passes to rightful heirs—children, parents, spouses, and other relatives. No one can arbitrarily decide to exclude a rightful heir or change the shares Allah has prescribed.\n\nIn early Islam, some people had made pacts of mutual support and inheritance with non-relatives. This verse instructs that such pledges should be honored. Some scholars say this was later abrogated when the full inheritance laws were revealed, while others say it refers to bequests (wasiyyah) that can be made to non-heirs up to one-third of the estate.\n\nAllah sees every agreement made, every promise kept or broken, and every right fulfilled or violated. Nothing escapes His knowledge, so fulfill your obligations knowing you are being watched.",
  "34": "The Arabic word 'qawwamun' means protectors, maintainers, and those responsible for leadership. This does not mean dictators or oppressors—it means men bear the primary responsibility for providing for, protecting, and caring for their families. With this authority comes accountability before Allah. A leader who abuses his position will be questioned on the Day of Judgment.\n\nTwo reasons are given for male leadership: (1) Allah has given men certain qualities that suit them for this role, and (2) men are obligated to spend their wealth on their families. A woman's earnings belong entirely to her; a man's earnings must support his wife and children. This financial obligation is the basis of his authority.\n\nRighteous wives are characterized by two qualities: (1) they are obedient—cooperating with their husbands in what is good (not in sin), and (2) they guard their chastity, their husband's honor, his wealth, and his secrets when he is absent. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said the best women are those who please their husbands when they look at them, obey them when they command them (in good), and do not go against their wishes regarding themselves or their wealth.\n\nNushuz (ill-conduct) refers to a wife's rebellion against her husband—arrogance, refusal to fulfill her duties, or defiance. The first step is gentle advice, reminding her of Allah and her responsibilities. Most problems can be solved through calm, loving communication.\n\nIf advice doesn't work, the husband may express his displeasure by sleeping separately. This is not abandoning the home but turning away in bed. This shows the seriousness of the situation without resorting to harsh measures.\n\nThis is the last resort after the previous two steps have failed. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained this must be light—not hitting the face, not causing injury, and not leaving marks. He also said: 'The best of you are those who are best to their wives, and I am the best of you to my wives.' Many scholars emphasize this should be symbolic at most, and the Prophet himself never struck any of his wives. He said: 'Do not strike the female servants of Allah.'\n\nOnce the wife returns to good conduct, the husband must not hold grudges, bring up past issues, or continue punishing her. Forgiveness and moving forward is required.\n\nThis reminder of Allah's supreme power is a warning to husbands: do not abuse your authority, for there is One far greater than you who sees everything and will hold you accountable for how you treat your wife.",
  "35": "When marital problems become serious and the couple cannot resolve them alone, the community should intervene through family mediation. Each side sends a trustworthy, wise representative who knows the couple and can advocate for reconciliation while understanding each person's perspective.\n\nThe key is sincere intention. If both spouses genuinely want to save the marriage, Allah will help them find a way. But if one or both are stubborn or have already decided to separate, even the best mediators cannot help. Reconciliation requires both parties to want it.\n\nAllah knows the true intentions in each person's heart—whether they sincerely want reconciliation or are just going through the motions. He is aware of every detail of the marital situation, even what neither spouse has expressed.",
  "36": "This is the foundation of Islam—Tawhid (monotheism). All worship must be directed exclusively to Allah. No partners, no intermediaries, no idols. This is the first and greatest commandment, the purpose for which humans were created.\n\nImmediately after mentioning His rights, Allah mentions parents' rights. This shows how important honoring parents is in Islam. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked: 'Which deed is most beloved to Allah?' He said: 'Prayer at its proper time.' He was asked: 'Then what?' He said: 'Kindness to parents.'\n\nExtended family members deserve kindness, support, and maintaining ties with them—even those who are difficult or who cut ties with you.\n\nChildren who have lost their fathers are vulnerable and need the community's care, support, and protection.\n\nThose who cannot meet their basic needs deserve charity and assistance from those whom Allah has blessed with wealth.\n\nThe Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Jibril kept recommending that I treat neighbors well until I thought he would make them heirs.' A 'near neighbor' could mean one who lives close by or one who is a relative; a 'distant neighbor' could mean one who lives further away or is not related. Both deserve good treatment.\n\nCompanions, colleagues, travel partners—anyone you spend time with deserves good treatment. This includes coworkers, classmates, and anyone in your company.\n\nTravelers who are stranded or in need deserve hospitality and assistance, even if they are wealthy at home. Travel makes people vulnerable.\n\nEven those in the lowest social positions must be treated with kindness. Islam improved the conditions of enslaved people and encouraged their freedom.\n\nAfter listing all these social obligations, Allah warns against the opposite attitude—arrogance and boasting. Those who think they are better than others, who show off their blessings and look down on the poor, orphans, and vulnerable—Allah does not love them.",
  "37": "Bad enough to be stingy yourself, but these people also encourage others to be stingy. They advise against charity, mock those who give, and spread a culture of hoarding rather than generosity.\n\nThey hide the blessings Allah has given them—whether wealth, knowledge, or other gifts—refusing to share or benefit others. This includes scholars who hide beneficial knowledge and wealthy people who refuse to give charity.\n\nExtreme stinginess and ingratitude can be signs of disbelief. Those who refuse to acknowledge Allah's blessings or fulfill their obligations face a humiliating punishment—appropriate for those whose arrogance prevented them from giving.",
  "38": "Spending for show (riya') destroys the reward of charity. These people give money, but their intention is to be praised by others, to gain reputation, or to show off their wealth—not to please Allah or help the needy.\n\nTheir showing off stems from not truly believing. If they believed in Allah, they would seek His pleasure alone. If they believed in the Last Day, they would know that only sincere deeds will be rewarded.\n\nSatan encourages showing off and corrupts sincere intentions. Those who follow his whispers and make him their companion will find he leads them only to destruction. On the Day of Judgment, Satan will abandon them completely.",
  "39": "This is a rhetorical question challenging those who refuse to believe. What would they have lost by believing? Faith only brings benefit—peace in this life and Paradise in the next. Their refusal harms no one but themselves.\n\nSimilarly, what would they have lost by giving charity? The wealth was from Allah anyway. Giving it in His cause would have brought them rewards far greater than what they gave. Their stinginess only deprives themselves.\n\nAllah knows their true reasons for disbelief and stinginess—whether it's arrogance, greed, or stubbornness. Nothing is hidden from Him.",
  "40": "Allah is absolutely just. He will not reduce anyone's good deeds by even the smallest amount, nor will He increase anyone's sins beyond what they committed. A dharrah (atom/speck) is used to indicate the smallest possible amount. This verse gives comfort to believers and warning to wrongdoers.\n\nNot only does Allah not reduce good deeds, He multiplies them—from ten times to seven hundred times to even more, depending on the sincerity and circumstances. This is pure divine generosity.\n\nBeyond the multiplication, Allah gives additional rewards from His own bounty—rewards that are not even earned but given as gifts. This is the generosity of the Most Merciful.",
  "41": "On the Day of Judgment, each nation will have a witness—their prophet—who will testify about whether they received the message and how they responded. No one will be able to claim they were not warned.\n\nProphet Muhammad (peace be upon him) will testify concerning his ummah. When this verse was recited to him, he wept, understanding the weight of this responsibility and the state of those who would reject the message. Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported that the Prophet asked him to recite Quran, and when he reached this verse, the Prophet said 'Enough' while tears flowed from his eyes.",
  "42": "The disbelievers will be so terrified and ashamed that they will wish they could simply disappear—that the earth would swallow them up or they could become dust like the ground they stand on. This is the ultimate regret.\n\nIn this world, people hide their sins, deny their wrongdoing, and lie their way out of consequences. On the Day of Judgment, nothing can be hidden. Allah knows everything, and even their own bodies will testify against them.",
  "43": "This verse was revealed before the complete prohibition of alcohol. It prohibited praying while drunk because prayer requires presence of mind, focus, and understanding of what one is saying. This was a gradual step toward the eventual total ban on intoxicants. The principle remains: approach prayer with a clear, focused mind.\n\nJanabah (major ritual impurity) occurs after sexual intercourse or emission. In this state, one must perform ghusl (full body bath) before praying. However, one may pass through a mosque without stopping if necessary.\n\nAllah provides concessions for difficult circumstances. If water is unavailable or using it would cause harm (such as for the sick), an alternative is provided.\n\nThis is tayammum—a symbolic purification using clean earth or dust when water is unavailable. It involves striking the earth with one's hands and wiping the face and hands. This shows Islam's practicality and consideration for various circumstances.\n\nAllah does not burden people beyond their capacity. He pardons shortcomings and forgives those who try their best within their circumstances.",
  "44": "This refers to some among the People of the Book (Jews and Christians) who had received divine revelation but did not follow it properly. They had knowledge of scripture but chose to misuse it.\n\nInstead of using their knowledge to find the truth, they traded it for falsehood. They chose misguidance despite knowing better—perhaps for worldly gain, pride, or stubbornness.\n\nNot content with their own misguidance, they actively work to lead Muslims astray. They want the believers to abandon the straight path and join them in error.",
  "45": "You may not always recognize who truly wishes you harm, but Allah knows every heart. Some enemies pretend to be friends; some apparent friends may have hidden enmity. Trust Allah's knowledge.\n\nWith Allah as your protector and helper, you need not fear any enemy. He is enough for those who trust in Him.",
  "46": "This verse describes how some people would twist words to insult the Prophet while appearing polite. 'Ra'ina' sounded like an Arabic word meaning 'give us attention' but in Hebrew could mean an insult. They would pretend to be respectful while actually mocking. They played word games to insult Islam and the Prophet.\n\nIf they had used straightforward, respectful language instead of wordplay, it would have been better for their own souls. Sincerity and respect, even in word choice, matter.\n\nTheir mockery and disrespect earned Allah's curse. However, 'except for a few' shows that not all were this way—some were sincere and did believe.",
  "47": "This is a direct call to Jews and Christians to accept the Quran, which confirms the truth in their own scriptures. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) was prophesied in their books; the Quran validates the original message of their prophets.\n\nThis is a warning of possible punishment—either physical (disfigurement) or metaphorical (being turned away from guidance and truth).\n\nReference to those Jews who violated the Sabbath and were transformed into apes as punishment (mentioned in Surah Al-Baqarah 2:65). This reminds them that Allah has punished rebellious communities before.\n\nWhen Allah decrees something, nothing can prevent it. His warnings should be taken seriously.",
  "48": "Shirk—worshipping anything or anyone besides Allah—is the only sin that Allah will not forgive if a person dies upon it without repentance. This includes worshipping idols, believing in multiple gods, or directing any act of worship to other than Allah. However, if a person repents from shirk before death, Allah can forgive even this.\n\nAll other sins, no matter how great, are subject to Allah's forgiveness. Murder, adultery, theft—any sin can be forgiven if Allah wills, whether through repentance or simply by His mercy. This gives hope to all sinners except those who die as polytheists.\n\nShirk is described as a 'grave sin' (ithm 'atheem) because it violates the most fundamental right—Allah's right to be worshipped alone. It is an offense against the Creator Himself and the very purpose of human existence.",
  "49": "Some people claim spiritual superiority, saying they are pure, chosen, or guaranteed paradise. This refers to those who boast about their spiritual status without basis.\n\nTrue purification and elevation come from Allah alone. He knows who is truly righteous and who is merely boastful. No one can declare themselves pure; only Allah can purify.\n\nThe 'fateel' is the thin thread-like fiber in the groove of a date stone—something almost invisible. Even this tiny amount will not be taken from anyone's rewards. Allah's justice is perfect.",
  "50": "Claiming special status with Allah without His authorization is lying about Him. Saying 'We are Allah's chosen people' or 'We will definitely go to paradise' without divine proof is fabrication.\n\nEven without other sins, just lying about Allah is enough to condemn a person. It's a clear, obvious sin that cannot be excused.",
  "51": "Some who received divine scripture abandoned its teachings to believe in 'jibt' (sorcery, idols) and 'taghut' (false deities, tyrants). Despite having knowledge, they chose falsehood.\n\nWorse still, they told the pagan Quraysh that their idol worship was better than Islam! This was said by some Jewish leaders who preferred the polytheists over the Muslims—a shocking betrayal of monotheistic principles for political reasons.",
  "52": "Allah's curse has fallen upon those who preferred polytheism over monotheism and declared idol-worshippers more guided than Muslims.\n\nWhen Allah curses someone, no one can protect them. All other helpers are powerless against Allah's decree.",
  "53": "This is a rhetorical question exposing their arrogance. Do they think they control Allah's kingdom and can decide who receives His blessings?\n\nIf they had any power over Allah's bounty, their stinginess would prevent them from giving anyone even the tiniest amount. The 'naqeer' is the speck in the groove of a date stone—something worthless. This exposes their mean character.",
  "54": "Their real problem is envy—they cannot accept that Allah has blessed Muhammad (peace be upon him) and the Muslims with prophethood and guidance. They wish these blessings were theirs alone.\n\nAllah reminds them that prophethood was never exclusive to one branch of Abraham's family. Allah gave scripture, wisdom, and authority to various descendants of Abraham—including Ishmael's line through Muhammad (peace be upon him). This is Allah's prerogative.",
  "55": "Even among the People of the Book, there was diversity—some accepted the truth while others rejected it. This shows that the door to faith was open, but many chose to close it.\n\nFor those who turn away out of arrogance and envy, Hell awaits. Its punishment is more than enough consequence for their rejection.",
  "56": "Those who deny Allah's revelations—whether the Quran, the signs in creation, or the miracles of the prophets—will face the Fire of Hell.\n\nThis vivid description shows the eternality and severity of the punishment. When skin burns, it loses sensation; so Allah will replace it with new skin so the punishment is continuously felt. This is justice for those who persistently rejected truth.\n\nAllah has the power to execute this punishment and the wisdom to assign it justly. No one escapes His might, and His decisions are always wise.",
  "57": "The believers who back up their faith with righteous actions will enter Paradise—gardens of unimaginable beauty with rivers flowing beneath them. This is the reward for faith and good deeds together.\n\nThe reward is eternal—not temporary like worldly pleasures. There is no end to the bliss of Paradise.\n\nThe spouses in Paradise are described as 'mutahharah'—purified from all physical and spiritual impurities. There will be no arguments, jealousy, menstruation, bodily impurities, or bad character—only perfect companionship.\n\nParadise offers perfect comfort—cool, pleasant shade that never ends. In contrast to the scorching sun of the Day of Judgment, the believers will enjoy perpetual comfort.",
  "58": "'Amanat' (trusts) includes everything entrusted to you—property, secrets, responsibilities, positions of authority. Everything must be returned or fulfilled faithfully. This verse was revealed when the Prophet (peace be upon him) took the keys of the Ka'bah from its custodian Uthman ibn Talhah after the conquest of Makkah, then returned them, establishing that trusteeships should be honored.\n\nThose in positions of judgment—whether rulers, judges, parents, or arbitrators—must be absolutely fair. No favoritism based on wealth, status, family, or personal feelings. Justice is a divine command.\n\nAllah praises His own commandment, emphasizing its excellence and importance. Fulfilling trusts and judging fairly are among the highest moral duties.\n\nAllah hears every dispute and sees every judgment. Nothing is hidden from Him, so judge as if He is watching—because He is.",
  "59": "Obedience to Allah and His Messenger is absolute and unconditional. The repetition of 'obey' emphasizes that the Messenger's authority is directly from Allah. Following the Sunnah is not optional.\n\nThis includes rulers, scholars, commanders, and anyone in legitimate authority. Obedience to them is required in matters that don't contradict Islamic law. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'There is no obedience to any creature if it involves disobedience to the Creator.'\n\nWhen disputes arise, the final authority is the Quran and Sunnah—not personal opinion, cultural tradition, or majority vote. This establishes Islamic governance and jurisprudence.\n\nReferring disputes to Allah and His Messenger is a test of faith. Those who truly believe will accept divine guidance over their own preferences.\n\nFollowing this method leads to the best outcomes in this world and the next. Human solutions may seem appealing, but divine guidance is always superior.",
  "60": "These are hypocrites who verbally claim faith in all divine revelations but their actions contradict their words.\n\nWhen disputes arose, they would go to pagan judges or unjust courts instead of the Prophet (peace be upon him), hoping for rulings that favored their desires. 'Taghut' refers to any false authority that leads away from Allah's law.\n\nTheir choice to seek judgment from other than Allah plays directly into Satan's plan to lead people astray. Every step away from divine guidance is a step deeper into misguidance.",
  "61": "When invited to accept divine judgment, the hypocrites refuse. Their physical turning away reflects the turning of their hearts from truth. They find excuses to avoid submitting to Allah's law because they know it may not favor their desires.",
  "62": "Their wrongdoing eventually catches up with them. When calamity strikes as a consequence of their actions, their hypocrisy becomes more apparent.\n\nAfter being caught or facing consequences, they come making excuses, swearing false oaths that they meant well. They claim they were just trying to find a middle ground or make peace. This is the behavior of hypocrites—denial and false justification when exposed.",
  "63": "Even if they deceive people with their words, Allah knows the reality of their hearts. No one can hide their true intentions from Him.\n\nThe Prophet is advised to (1) not let their behavior distract him, (2) warn them of consequences, and (3) speak to them in a way that penetrates their hearts—words that are eloquent, serious, and reach deep into their conscience. Sometimes harsh truth is necessary for someone drowning in self-deception.",
  "64": "The purpose of sending messengers is that they be followed, not just heard. A messenger rejected is a mission failed. Obedience to prophets is obedience to Allah.\n\nThe door of repentance remains open. If the hypocrites sincerely repented for their wrongs and came to the Prophet asking him to pray for their forgiveness...\n\nAllah would have forgiven them. This shows the vastness of divine mercy and the value of the Prophet's intercession. But they must come sincerely, not with excuses.",
  "65": "Allah swears by Himself—a most serious oath—that true faith requires accepting the Prophet's judgment. Not just externally, but as the final authority in all matters of dispute.\n\nTrue faith means no inner resentment when the Prophet rules. The heart should be at peace with his judgment even if it goes against one's desires. This is the test of sincere faith.\n\nComplete submission (tasleem) is required—not grudging compliance, not partial acceptance, but wholehearted surrender to the Prophet's rulings. This is what it means to truly believe.",
  "66": "Allah reveals how weak their faith really is. If they struggle to accept simple rulings, imagine if they were commanded with severe tests like those given to previous nations. Only the truly sincere few would obey.\n\nFollowing divine guidance—even when difficult—always leads to better outcomes. Obedience strengthens faith and brings inner peace, while disobedience brings anxiety and weakness.",
  "67": "Had they obeyed, Allah would have rewarded them generously—not just according to their deeds but with additional bounty from His grace. Obedience opens doors of divine reward.",
  "68": "Obedience leads to more guidance. Those who follow what they know are given more knowledge and clearer direction. The straight path becomes increasingly clear to those who walk upon it.",
  "69": "This is one of the most beautiful promises in the Quran. Obedience to Allah and His Messenger earns companionship with the greatest people in Paradise.\n\nThe highest rank—those chosen by Allah to deliver His message to humanity.\n\nAs-Siddiqeen are those who always speak the truth, accept the truth immediately, and live by the truth. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) earned this title for his immediate acceptance of the Prophet's message.\n\nAsh-Shuhada are those who died in Allah's cause. They are alive with their Lord, provided for, and honored.\n\nAs-Saliheen are the general righteous believers who did good deeds throughout their lives.\n\nWhat better companions could one ask for? This verse was revealed when a companion was sad thinking about being separated from the Prophet in Paradise, and Allah reassured the believers that obedience ensures eternal companionship with the best of creation.",
  "70": "Reaching such company is not due to one's merit alone but is Allah's grace and favor. No one earns Paradise through deeds alone; it is ultimately Allah's mercy.\n\nAllah knows who is truly sincere in their obedience and who deserves this honor. Nothing is hidden from Him.",
  "71": "When facing enemies, believers must be prepared. This includes military readiness, intelligence gathering, strategic planning, and maintaining vigilance. Faith does not mean recklessness.\n\nDepending on the situation, believers may advance in smaller units or as a unified force. This shows tactical flexibility while maintaining collective effort.",
  "72": "This exposes the hypocrites who find excuses to avoid participating in legitimate struggle. They hang back while others sacrifice.\n\nIf the believers suffer losses, the hypocrites congratulate themselves on their 'wisdom' in staying behind. They see avoiding sacrifice as divine favor, when in reality their cowardice deprives them of honor.",
  "73": "When the believers return victorious with booty and honor, the same hypocrites express regret for not joining! They want the benefits without the sacrifice. They speak 'as if there had been no bond'—meaning they show no loyalty when it costs them something, but suddenly claim closeness when there is profit. This exposes their true motivation: worldly gain, not faith.",
  "74": "True believers prioritize eternal reward over temporary worldly comfort. They are willing to sacrifice their lives for Allah's cause. This is not glorifying war but encouraging those who believe to stand up for justice and truth.\n\nBoth outcomes are victories. If killed, they become martyrs with all its rewards. If victorious, they have honored Allah's cause. Either way, a great reward awaits. There is no losing when fighting for Allah.",
  "75": "This is a strong reproach. What excuse could there be for not fighting when the cause is just?\n\nFighting in Islam is not about conquest or domination but about liberating the oppressed. When innocent men, women, and children are being persecuted and cannot defend themselves, those who are able have a responsibility to help them. This is jihad in its truest sense—struggle against injustice.\n\nThe oppressed are calling out to Allah, begging for rescue from those who persecute them. Their cry should move believers to action.\n\nThe oppressed seek a 'wali' (protector/guardian) and 'naseer' (helper) from Allah. This du'a shows their trust in Allah to send someone to deliver them. Throughout Islamic history, Allah has answered such prayers by raising up those who fought for the rights of the oppressed.",
  "76": "Those who truly believe fight for a noble purpose—to establish Allah's justice, protect the oppressed, defend their faith, and earn Allah's pleasure. Their motivation is not worldly gain, nationalism, or personal glory, but solely the cause of Allah. This purifies their intention and elevates their struggle.\n\nThose who oppose truth fight for 'taghut'—false deities, tyrannical systems, and ultimately Satan's agenda. Whether they realize it or not, those who fight against Allah's cause are serving Satan's interests. Their struggle, no matter how powerful it appears, is fundamentally misguided.\n\nThe command is clear: confront those who serve Satan's agenda. The 'awliya' (allies/forces) of Satan are those who do his bidding, spreading corruption and oppression. Believers should not be intimidated by them.\n\nThis is a tremendous source of comfort and courage for believers. No matter how elaborate Satan's plots appear, they are fundamentally weak because they are built on falsehood. Allah's plan always prevails. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that Satan flees when he hears the adhan. A being that runs from a simple call to prayer cannot withstand those who truly rely on Allah. When believers trust in Allah, Satan's tricks crumble.",
  "77": "In the early Makkan period, Muslims were persecuted but commanded to be patient and not retaliate. Some companions, eager to defend themselves, would ask for permission to fight. They were told to restrain themselves and focus on spiritual development through prayer and charity.\n\nWhen permission to fight finally came in Madinah, a strange reversal happened. Some of the same people who had been eager to fight now became afraid. They feared the enemy armies more than they feared Allah—or even more intensely! This exposes a weakness in their faith. When fighting was forbidden, they wanted it; when it was permitted, they feared it. This inconsistency reveals that their earlier eagerness may not have been purely for Allah's sake.\n\nThey questioned Allah's wisdom and wished for delay. 'Just a little more time to enjoy life,' they thought. This reveals attachment to worldly comfort over obedience to Allah. They were not ready to sacrifice because their hearts were still tied to this temporary world.\n\nThe Prophet is commanded to remind them of reality. All worldly pleasures—wealth, comfort, safety, long life—are temporary and insignificant compared to eternity. Even if one lived a hundred years in luxury, it is nothing compared to endless time in the Hereafter.\n\nFor those with taqwa (God-consciousness), the Hereafter holds rewards beyond imagination—eternal paradise, Allah's pleasure, and the company of the righteous. Why cling to something temporary when something infinitely better awaits?\n\nThe 'fateel' is the thin thread in a date stone—almost worthless. Allah assures that not even this tiny amount will be unjustly taken from anyone's rewards. Every sacrifice, every moment of fear overcome for Allah's sake, will be fully compensated. Nothing is lost with Allah.",
  "78": "This verse destroys the illusion that avoiding battle preserves life. Death is decreed by Allah and will find everyone at their appointed time, regardless of where they hide. Whether in the strongest fortress, the safest bunker, or the most remote location—when your time comes, nothing can delay it. So why let fear of death prevent you from doing what is right? The coward dies at the same time as the brave; the difference is how they lived.\n\nWhen the hypocrites experience good fortune—victory, wealth, health—they acknowledge it as Allah's blessing. This much is correct.\n\nHowever, when hardship strikes—defeat, loss, suffering—they blame the Prophet (peace be upon him) and the believers. They said the presence of Muslims brought bad luck. This is the attitude of hypocrites throughout history: take credit for good, blame others for bad.\n\nThe Prophet is told to clarify: everything—good and bad—occurs by Allah's decree. Nothing happens without His permission. This is the Islamic understanding of qadr (divine destiny). Allah is in control of all affairs.\n\nAllah expresses amazement at their lack of understanding. Despite having the truth explained to them, they refuse to grasp basic concepts of faith. Their problem is not intellectual but spiritual—their hearts are sealed due to their hypocrisy.",
  "79": "Every blessing, success, and good fortune comes from Allah's grace and mercy. Even our ability to do good deeds is a gift from Him. We should never attribute our successes purely to our own abilities but recognize Allah's favor.\n\nThis complements the previous verse. While everything happens by Allah's decree, the calamities we face are often consequences of our own sins and mistakes. Allah allows hardship to come as a result of our choices. This teaches personal responsibility—don't blame others or even divine destiny for problems that stem from your own actions. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'A person is deprived of provision because of sins they commit.' This verse encourages self-reflection when facing difficulties.\n\nMuhammad (peace be upon him) was not sent only to the Arabs or to a specific nation, but to all of humanity until the Day of Judgment. His message is universal—every human being is addressed by his prophethood.\n\nAllah witnesses the Prophet's delivery of the message and witnesses how people respond. No one can deny receiving the message when Allah Himself is the Witness.",
  "80": "This is a foundational principle of Islam. Obedience to the Prophet (peace be upon him) is not separate from obedience to Allah—it IS obedience to Allah. The Prophet's commands, teachings, and Sunnah carry divine authority. Those who claim to follow Allah but reject the Prophet's guidance are deceiving themselves. You cannot have one without the other.\n\nThe Prophet's duty is to deliver the message, not to force people to accept it. Those who turn away are responsible for their own choices. This relieves the Prophet of feeling responsible for everyone's guidance—he did his job; their rejection is on them. This also establishes that faith cannot be compelled.",
  "81": "In the Prophet's presence, the hypocrites would declare their obedience and loyalty. Their words sounded perfect—respectful, agreeable, compliant.\n\nThe moment they left the Prophet's company, their true nature emerged. 'Bayyata' means they would spend the night plotting and scheming against what they had publicly agreed to. Their nights were spent in conspiracy, their days in pretense. This is the essence of hypocrisy: saying one thing publicly and doing the opposite privately.\n\nNothing is hidden from Allah. Every whispered plot, every secret meeting, every contradictory plan is recorded. They may think their nighttime conspiracies are secret, but Allah documents everything. This is both a warning to hypocrites and comfort to believers—Allah knows who is sincere and who is not.\n\nDon't let their schemes consume your attention or cause you distress. Recognize them for what they are and don't be disturbed by their plotting.\n\nTawakkul (reliance on Allah) is the believer's response to hypocrites and enemies. Rather than anxiety and counter-scheming, place your affairs in Allah's hands. He is the best planner.\n\nAllah is 'wakeel'—the One who takes care of all affairs for those who trust Him. When you have Allah managing your affairs, you need no other protector or strategist. He is enough.",
  "82": "This is a powerful invitation to engage deeply with the Quran. 'Tadabbur' means to ponder, contemplate, and reflect carefully—not just to read or recite superficially. Allah challenges people to think critically about the Quran. True reflection leads to recognizing its divine origin. The Quran welcomes scrutiny because it has nothing to hide. Those who reflect sincerely will find guidance; those who approach it with closed hearts will remain blind.\n\nThis is a remarkable challenge. The Quran was revealed over 23 years, in different places, addressing various situations, covering law, history, science, spirituality, and prophecy—yet it contains no contradictions. Human works, no matter how carefully edited, contain inconsistencies over time. Authors change views, make mistakes, or contradict themselves. The Quran's perfect internal harmony—despite being revealed piecemeal over two decades—is evidence of its divine source. Every claim is consistent, every teaching harmonious, every prophecy accurate. This consistency is impossible for human authorship.",
  "83": "Some people, when they hear any news—whether reassuring or alarming—immediately spread it without verification. They share rumors about victories, defeats, threats, or safety without confirming the truth. This behavior causes confusion, panic, or false hope. In times of war especially, unverified information can be devastating. This verse addresses a timeless problem that is even more relevant in our age of instant social media sharing.\n\nThe proper approach is to take unverified news to those qualified to assess it—leaders, scholars, experts who can investigate and determine what is true. 'Istinbat' means to extract the truth, to investigate properly. Not everyone is qualified to evaluate sensitive information. This establishes a principle: refer matters to those with knowledge and authority rather than spreading unconfirmed reports.\n\nWithout Allah's guidance and protection, people would easily fall into Satan's traps—spreading lies, causing panic, and creating division. Only by Allah's mercy are believers saved from these pitfalls. The 'few' who would have been saved refers to those with exceptional wisdom and self-control, showing how rare such qualities are without divine assistance.",
  "84": "This is a direct command to the Prophet (peace be upon him) to engage in legitimate struggle for Allah's cause. Fighting here is defensive—protecting the Muslim community and establishing justice.\n\nThis profound statement establishes individual responsibility. Even if others refuse to fight, the Prophet must do his duty. He is not responsible for forcing others to join; he is responsible for his own obedience to Allah. This principle applies to all believers: you cannot control others' choices, but you are responsible for your own. Do your duty regardless of how many join you.\n\nWhile not responsible for others' choices, the Prophet should still encourage and inspire them. 'Tahreed' means to urge, motivate, and inspire. Leadership involves both personal example and encouraging others to excellence.\n\nThe outcome is in Allah's hands. Fighting with faith may cause Allah to restrain the enemy's power. Victory comes from Allah, not from numbers or weapons alone.\n\nNo matter how powerful the enemy appears, Allah is infinitely more powerful. His might ('ba's') and His punishment ('tankeel') surpass anything humans can muster. This provides courage to believers facing seemingly overwhelming odds.",
  "85": "If you help someone achieve something good—whether by recommendation, support, advocacy, or assistance—you share in the reward of that good. This encourages Muslims to be helpful, to use their influence for good causes, to recommend people for jobs they deserve, to support charitable efforts, and to facilitate good in any way possible. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Intercede and you will be rewarded.' Even if your intercession is not accepted, you are rewarded for trying.\n\nThe reverse is equally true. If you help someone commit wrong—by recommending an unqualified person, supporting injustice, covering for wrongdoers, or facilitating sin—you share in the sin. This should make people careful about what they support, recommend, or facilitate. Don't use your influence for evil.\n\n'Muqeet' means the One who witnesses, maintains, and has power over all things. Allah sees every intercession—good or bad—and will recompense accordingly. Nothing escapes His knowledge.",
  "86": "Islam emphasizes good manners in all interactions, including something as simple as greetings. When someone says 'Assalamu Alaikum' (Peace be upon you), you should respond with 'Wa Alaikum Assalam wa Rahmatullahi wa Barakatuh' (And upon you be peace, and Allah's mercy and blessings)—adding to what they said. At minimum, return the same greeting. Never respond with less than what was given. This builds a society of mutual respect and warmth. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I tell you something that if you do it, you will love one another? Spread the greeting of peace among yourselves.'\n\nEven something as small as returning a greeting is recorded by Allah. He takes account of everything—the small courtesies as well as the major deeds. This should motivate believers to perfect even the smallest aspects of their character.",
  "87": "This is the declaration of Tawhid—absolute monotheism. Allah alone deserves worship; there is no deity besides Him. This fundamental truth is the foundation of Islam and the message of every prophet.\n\nResurrection is certain. Every human being who ever lived will be gathered before Allah for accounting. The word 'la-yajma'annakum' contains emphasis (the lam of oath and the nun of emphasis), making this an emphatic promise.\n\nThe Day of Judgment is not a possibility or a theory—it is an absolute certainty. Allah removes any room for doubt. Those who deny it do so against clear evidence and divine declaration.\n\nThis rhetorical question establishes Allah as the ultimate source of truth. No one's word is more reliable, more honest, or more certain than Allah's. When Allah makes a promise, it will absolutely be fulfilled. If Allah says the Day of Judgment will come, it will come without any doubt.",
  "88": "This verse addresses a situation that arose among the early Muslims in Madinah. There was a group of people who outwardly claimed Islam but whose commitment was questionable—they may have been hypocrites or weak in faith. The Muslim community became divided into two camps about how to deal with them: one group wanted to treat them as believers and be gentle with them, while another group recognized their hypocrisy and wanted to treat them as enemies. Allah questions why the believers are divided over people whose status Allah has already made clear. This teaches us several lessons: (1) We should not be divided over clear matters; (2) We should refer to Allah and His Messenger for clarity rather than following our own opinions; (3) Unity is important, and division weakens the community. The division likely stemmed from some people's tribal loyalties, personal relationships, or wishful thinking that these individuals might genuinely accept Islam.\n\nAllah explains that He has caused these people to return to their state of disbelief (arkasahum—literally 'turned them upside down' or 'caused them to relapse') as a consequence of their own evil deeds and choices. When people persist in hypocrisy, lying, and insincerity, Allah withdraws His guidance and leaves them in their misguidance. This is divine justice, not oppression—they chose the path of deception, so Allah allowed them to continue on it. Their own sins became the cause of their spiritual downfall. This is a warning to all of us: persistent sin can lead to the heart being sealed and the person being unable to find guidance. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'When a servant commits a sin, a black spot appears on his heart. If he repents, it is polished away, but if he persists, it increases until it covers his entire heart.' (Tirmidhi)\n\nThis is a rhetorical question expressing the futility of trying to guide someone whom Allah has abandoned to misguidance because of their own choices. If Allah has left someone to stray as a consequence of their persistent rejection, how can you hope to guide them? This does not mean we should not call people to Islam or that we can know for certain who is beyond guidance—rather, it means that ultimate guidance is in Allah's hands, not ours. We should do our duty of conveying the message, but not be distressed if some people refuse to accept it. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was deeply saddened when people rejected Islam, and Allah consoled him multiple times, reminding him that his job is to deliver the message, not to force people to believe.\n\nThis is a definitive statement: if Allah has abandoned someone to misguidance (as a just consequence of their own persistent choice of evil), no human effort can guide them. No amount of preaching, arguing, or pleading will change their heart. Only Allah can guide hearts, and if He withholds guidance as a punishment for someone's arrogance and sin, they will remain lost. This should make us realize: (1) Guidance is entirely from Allah, and we are merely means; (2) We should pray for Allah to guide people rather than relying solely on our arguments; (3) We should not exhaust ourselves trying to guide those who have clearly sealed their hearts; (4) We should fear falling into the state where Allah withdraws His guidance from us, and thus constantly seek His guidance and repent from sins.",
  "89": "The hypocrites and enemies of Islam do not merely want to remain in their own disbelief—they actively wish for Muslims to also become disbelievers. They want everyone to be at the same level of misguidance because: (1) It would validate their own choices; (2) It would eliminate the 'judgment' they feel when confronted with sincere believers; (3) Misery loves company—those who are lost want others to be lost with them. This is similar to Shaitan, who does not just disobey Allah himself but works tirelessly to make humans disobey as well. This teaches us to be cautious about the company we keep and the influences we allow into our lives. Those who have abandoned the truth often try to pull others down with them, sometimes subtly through mockery, peer pressure, or making sin seem normal and acceptable.\n\nMuslims are commanded not to take these hypocrites or uncertain individuals as close allies, confidants, or protectors until they prove their sincerity by making hijrah (emigration) in the path of Allah. At that time in early Islamic history, emigrating from Makkah to Madinah was a clear test of faith—it required leaving home, wealth, and family for the sake of Islam. Those who made this sacrifice demonstrated their genuine commitment. Today, hijrah in its physical sense may not always be obligatory, but the principle remains: true alliance should be with those who demonstrate genuine commitment to Islam. The broader meaning of hijrah includes leaving sin for obedience, leaving bad company for good company, and sacrificing worldly comforts for faith. We should be cautious about making close alliances with those whose commitment to Islam is questionable or who may lead us away from the straight path.\n\nIf these individuals refuse to emigrate and instead turn away from Islam, openly opposing the Muslims or joining the enemy, then they are to be treated as enemy combatants in the context of war. This is not a general command to kill all who refuse to emigrate, but specifically refers to those who show their true colors by actively fighting against Muslims or joining the ranks of the enemy. This must be understood in its historical context: the early Muslim community was in a state of war with the Quraysh and their allies, and these commands relate to dealing with traitors and active enemies during wartime. Islamic law has detailed rules about warfare, peace treaties, and treatment of non-combatants. Verses must be understood in context, not isolated and misapplied.\n\nMuslims are again warned not to take these active enemies as allies or helpers in any matter. This is a matter of communal security and spiritual protection—allying with those who wish to destroy Islam would be self-destructive. The principle extends beyond warfare: we should be cautious about who we trust with important matters, who we seek help from, and who we allow to influence our decisions, especially regarding our faith and community.",
  "90": "Allah provides exceptions to the command to fight: those who seek refuge with a nation that has a peace treaty with the Muslims are to be left alone. Even in times of conflict, treaties must be honored, and those who are protected by such agreements are not to be harmed. This demonstrates Islam's emphasis on honoring commitments and respecting agreements, even in war.\n\nAnother exception is made for those who are clearly not interested in fighting—they neither want to fight the Muslims nor fight alongside their own people against the Muslims. Their 'hearts are constricted' about fighting; they are conflicted and prefer to remain neutral. Such people demonstrate by their restraint that they are not a threat. Islam does not require us to fight those who are not fighting us.\n\nAllah reminds the believers that He is in control of all affairs. If He had willed, He could have given these people the power and motivation to fight against the Muslims, but He did not. This should inspire gratitude—recognize that even the neutrality of some enemies is from Allah's mercy and protection. It should also inspire humility—our victories and safety are from Allah, not merely from our own strength.\n\nThis is a clear command: if people refrain from hostility and offer peace, Muslims are not permitted to attack them. This demolishes the false claim that Islam spread by forcing everyone to convert or die. Islam only permits fighting against those who are actively fighting Muslims or oppressing them. Those who seek peace and refrain from aggression are to be left in peace. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Do not wish to meet the enemy, but if you meet them, be patient.' (Bukhari & Muslim) Muslims should prefer peace but be ready to defend themselves when necessary.",
  "91": "This verse describes a different group—deceptive people who try to play both sides. They show friendship to Muslims when with them, claiming they do not want conflict, but they also maintain loyalty to the enemies of Islam, participating in hostility when away from Muslims. They want security from both sides while being loyal to neither. These are the most dangerous type because they are two-faced traitors who spy, spread misinformation, and wait for opportunities to betray the Muslims.\n\nWhenever they return to their own people or are tempted back into hostility and disbelief, they plunge into it headfirst. They are not genuinely neutral or peaceful—they are opportunistic and deceptive, waiting for the right moment to show their true allegiance. Their restraint around Muslims is not from genuine neutrality but from fear or seeking advantage.\n\nThe test is clear: do they genuinely keep their distance, offer peace, and refrain from all hostility? If not—if they continue to maintain connections with your enemies, participate in plots against you, or engage in any form of aggression—then they have shown their true nature.\n\nThose who fail this test—who do not keep away, do not offer peace, and do not refrain from hostility—are to be treated as active enemies in war. This is not about peaceful people or even those who are genuinely neutral, but about active fifth columnists and traitors who pose a real threat to the Muslim community's security.\n\nAllah has given the Muslim community clear authority (sultan mubin—evident authorization) to deal with these treacherous individuals. This is not vigilante justice but divinely sanctioned authority to protect the community from internal threats and traitors. In modern terms, this would relate to laws against treason, espionage, and collaboration with the enemy during wartime. The principle is that a community has the right to defend itself against those who seek to destroy it from within.",
  "92": "Allah establishes a fundamental principle: it is absolutely not permissible for one Muslim to intentionally kill another Muslim. The only exception mentioned here is accidental killing (khata'—unintentional, by mistake). The sanctity of a Muslim's life is emphasized throughout Islamic teachings. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'The termination of a believer's life is more significant to Allah than the destruction of the entire world.' (Nasai) He also said during his Farewell Pilgrimage: 'Your blood, your property, and your honor are sacred to you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this city of yours.' (Bukhari & Muslim) Intentional murder of a believer is one of the gravest sins in Islam, second only to shirk (associating partners with Allah).\n\nIf the killing was genuinely accidental (for example, in a hunting accident, a traffic accident, or a stray shot in permissible warfare), the killer owes two things: (1) Kaffarah (expiation): freeing a believing slave. In the past, this was the primary way; today, scholars discuss alternatives since formal slavery has been abolished, possibly including freeing someone from unjust imprisonment or debt, or other equivalent acts of charity; (2) Diyah (blood money): financial compensation paid to the victim's family. The amount is determined by Islamic law and was traditionally equivalent to 100 camels or their value in gold. However, the family has the right to forgive this debt charitably. These requirements serve multiple purposes: they provide support for the victim's family, they serve as a deterrent and reminder of the gravity of taking a life even accidentally, and they require the person to perform a significant act of worship and charity to atone for their mistake.\n\nIf the victim is a Muslim who lived among a non-Muslim people who are at war with the Muslims, and the killing was accidental (perhaps in the confusion of battle), the killer must still free a believing slave but is not required to pay blood money to the enemy people. This is because paying money to an enemy nation would strengthen them against the Muslims. However, the spiritual atonement of freeing a slave is still required because a Muslim life was taken.\n\nIf the victim belongs to a people who have a peace treaty with the Muslims—whether the victim himself is Muslim or not, depending on scholarly interpretations—then both requirements must be fulfilled: blood money is paid to the family (because the treaty must be honored and justice served) and a believing slave must be freed (as atonement). This shows Islam's commitment to honoring treaties and providing justice even across religious and national lines.\n\nIf the person who caused the accidental death cannot afford to free a slave (or in modern times, cannot fulfill the equivalent), they must fast for two consecutive months without breaking the sequence. This fasting is described as 'tawbatan min Allah' (repentance accepted by Allah), meaning it is a divinely prescribed way to seek Allah's forgiveness for this grave mistake. The consecutive nature (any interruption requires starting over, except for valid excuses like menstruation or illness) makes it a significant act of devotion that requires discipline and commitment. This serves as atonement and a constant reminder throughout those months of the gravity of having taken a life.\n\nAllah knows all circumstances, intentions, and details of every case. His rulings are based on perfect knowledge. He is also All-Wise, and His legislation serves wise purposes even when we might not fully understand all the wisdom behind it. These rulings about accidental killing perfectly balance justice for the victim's family, atonement for the killer, and the broader interests of society.",
  "93": "This verse addresses deliberate, intentional murder of a Muslim. The punishment mentioned is extraordinarily severe: Hell, with emphasis on remaining there (khalidan fiha). Some scholars have debated whether this means eternal stay or a very long time, as there is a principle in Islamic theology that a Muslim who dies with even a mustard seed of faith will eventually exit Hell, even if after immense punishment. However, the threat is clear and severe—intentionally murdering a believer is among the gravest of major sins, and its punishment is terrifying. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about someone who kills a believer intentionally and then repents and believes and does righteous deeds. He said: 'How can he repent? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying: The murdered believer will come on the Day of Judgment holding the head of his killer, with his veins dripping blood, saying: O Lord, ask him why he killed me! And Allah says: He has forbidden Paradise to him.' (Nasai) However, other scholars mention that repentance is possible for all sins if it is sincere, as Allah says: 'Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, despair not of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins.' (Quran 39:53) But this requires genuine, profound repentance, seeking forgiveness from the victim's family, accepting worldly punishment if applicable, and complete transformation.\n\nThree punishments are mentioned: Allah's anger (ghadab)—His wrath and displeasure; La'nah (being cursed)—being driven away from Allah's mercy; and a prepared punishment waiting for them. These spiritual and eternal consequences make this sin one of the most serious. Allah's anger is more terrifying than any worldly punishment because it means being in enmity with the Creator of all things.\n\nBeyond the Hellfire already mentioned, Allah has prepared 'adhaban 'azhiman'—a tremendous, enormous punishment specifically for those who murder believers. This emphasizes the severity multiplied. The deliberate taking of an innocent Muslim life is treated with utmost seriousness in Islam because: (1) It violates the fundamental right to life that Allah has made sacred; (2) It destroys a worshipper of Allah; (3) It tears apart families and communities; (4) It spreads fear and disorder; (5) It is an act of supreme arrogance, playing God over someone's life. This verse should make every Muslim extremely cautious about any action that might lead to taking an innocent life, and it completely forbids vigilante justice, revenge killings, and murder motivated by anger, greed, or any other worldly reason.",
  "94": "Allah commands Muslims to exercise great caution and verification (tabayyanū) when they are fighting in His cause. Do not rush to attack or kill without being certain about the status of the person. This verse was revealed after an incident: A group of Muslims encountered a man from a tribe that had previously been hostile. When the Muslims approached him, he said 'As-salamu alaikum' (peace be upon you—the Islamic greeting). One of the Companions thought he was saying this only to protect himself, not out of genuine faith, so he killed him and took his property. When they returned to the Prophet (peace be upon him), this verse was revealed, rebuking this hasty action. The command is to be very careful and verify before taking any life, even in the context of war.\n\nIf someone offers the Islamic greeting of peace, accept it and do not accuse them of lying or faking their faith. Do not say 'You are not a believer' and then kill them. Their outward profession of Islam should be accepted, and we should not appoint ourselves as judges of hearts. Only Allah knows what is truly in hearts. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'I have been commanded to fight people until they say: There is no deity except Allah. When they say it, their blood and property are protected from me, except by its right, and their reckoning is with Allah.' (Bukhari & Muslim) Once someone makes even an outward profession of faith, their life becomes sacred and cannot be taken without legitimate Islamic legal cause (like committing murder or apostasy combined with treason).\n\nThe verse identifies the real motivation behind such hasty killing: greed for worldly possessions. The Companion who killed the man took his belongings. Allah exposes this as the true motive—not zeal for Islam but desire for material gain disguised as religious justification. This is a stern warning against letting worldly desires corrupt our actions, especially in religious contexts. How often do people commit sins or injustices while claiming they are for religion or principle, when in reality they are motivated by greed, pride, or other base desires?\n\nRather than seeking the meager worldly possessions of one person through such a grave sin, trust that Allah has abundant rewards and bounties available for those who obey Him. The spoils of lawful war, the provisions in this life, and above all, the eternal rewards of the Hereafter are far greater than anything you might gain through hastily killing someone who professes Islam. Why risk Allah's punishment for such a small gain when Allah has infinite treasures?\n\nRemember that you too were once outside of Islam. You were once ignorant, perhaps hostile, or uncertain. Then Allah guided you and blessed you with Islam. So have mercy and patience with others who may be newly entering Islam or who may be uncertain in their commitment. Do not be arrogant as though you were always guided. Guidance is a gift from Allah, and He can guide anyone. The man you killed might have been sincerely embracing Islam just as you once did. This reminder should create humility and empathy.\n\nThe command to verify and be certain is repeated for emphasis. Take your time, investigate, be cautious. And remember that Allah is fully aware of all your actions—your true intentions, your motivations, and your deeds. You might fool people, but you cannot fool Allah. The fact that He sees and knows everything should make us extremely careful in all our actions, especially in grave matters like taking a life. The Prophet (peace be upon him) severely rebuked the Companion who committed this error, saying: 'Did you tear open his heart to see what was inside?' (Bukhari & Muslim), emphasizing that we judge by the outward and leave the inward to Allah.",
  "95": "Allah makes a clear distinction between those who actively strive in His cause (mujahidun) and those who remain behind without valid reason. The strivers give both their wealth (financing the effort, equipping soldiers, supporting families of fighters) and their lives (risking death on the battlefield). Those who stay home without excuse have not made this sacrifice. The 'valid excuses' include: blindness, chronic illness, severe disability, extreme poverty, or other conditions that genuinely prevent participation. Such people are not blamed. However, those who are capable but simply choose comfort over sacrifice are not equal to the strivers.\n\nEven above those who have valid excuses for not fighting, the mujahidun are elevated in rank. This is not to condemn those with excuses—they are not sinful—but to emphasize the exceptional virtue of those who sacrifice. The one who is blind or ill cannot help their condition, but the one who sacrifices despite being able to stay safe demonstrates a higher level of commitment. This verse was revealed and Ibn Umm Maktum, a blind Companion, was present with the Prophet (peace be upon him). He asked: 'O Messenger of Allah, do you see any excuse for me?' Then the exception 'other than those with valid excuses' was added to the verse. (Bukhari)\n\nBoth groups—those who stay with valid excuses and those who strive—are promised Paradise (al-husna—the best, i.e., Paradise). Those who stay behind with valid excuses are not sinful and will receive Allah's reward based on their intentions and whatever contributions they can make. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever sincerely asks Allah for martyrdom, Allah will grant him the status of a martyr even if he dies in his bed.' (Muslim)\n\nWhile both receive good rewards, the strivers receive a far greater reward. Their active sacrifice, their risk of life, and their giving of wealth earn them special stations in Paradise far beyond what others receive. This is divine justice—greater effort and sacrifice yield greater reward. This should motivate us to always seek the highest levels of good deeds rather than settling for the minimum.",
  "96": "The greater reward is specified: (1) Darajat—elevated ranks in Paradise. Paradise has levels, and those who strive are in the highest levels, nearest to Allah; (2) Maghfirah—complete forgiveness of all sins. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Martyrdom expiates everything except debt.' (Muslim); (3) Rahmah—special mercy from Allah, His pleasure and closeness. These three gifts are beyond price. To be in the highest Paradise, to have every sin forgiven, and to receive Allah's special mercy—this is worth every sacrifice.\n\nThe verse concludes with two of Allah's beautiful names: Al-Ghafur (the Most Forgiving) and Ar-Rahim (the Most Merciful). Despite all our shortcomings and sins, Allah's forgiveness is vast, and His mercy encompasses everything. For those who strive sincerely, even if imperfectly, His forgiveness covers their flaws, and His mercy grants them far more than they deserve. This should give us hope and motivation—no matter how inadequate we feel, if we sincerely strive for Allah's sake, His mercy and forgiveness will embrace us. These verses about jihad (striving) have broad application: while they specifically addressed military struggle in the early Muslim community's context, the principles apply to all forms of striving for Allah—seeking knowledge, calling to Islam, supporting the faith financially, raising righteous children, serving the community, and sacrificing comfort for faith. Whatever we sacrifice in Allah's cause, He will reward immeasurably.",
  "97": "At death, the angels will question those who lived among disbelievers without trying to practice their faith or escape. 'Wronged themselves' means they harmed their own souls by remaining in a situation where they couldn't practice Islam. The question 'What were you doing?' demands an explanation for their choices.\n\nTheir excuse is that they were weak and oppressed—unable to practice Islam openly or resist the pressure around them. They claim they had no choice.\n\nThe angels reject this excuse. Allah's earth is vast. If one place is hostile to your faith, move to another. This establishes the principle that Muslims should not remain in situations where they cannot practice their religion if they have the ability to leave. Emigration (hijrah) becomes obligatory when one cannot practice Islam and has the means to relocate.\n\nThose who could have emigrated but chose comfort over faith will face Hell. This is a severe warning against choosing worldly ease over religious commitment when alternatives exist.",
  "98": "This exception shows Allah's perfect justice. Those who are genuinely unable to emigrate—the truly weak, the imprisoned, the elderly, women without male guardians to travel with, children, the poor who lack resources—are not blamed. They lack both the means ('heelah' - resources, strategy) and the knowledge of how to emigrate ('sabeel' - a way, path). Their situation is genuinely beyond their control.",
  "99": "For the genuinely helpless, there is hope of Allah's pardon. The word ''asa' (it is hoped/expected) from Allah indicates strong likelihood—when Allah expresses hope for something, it essentially means He will do it.\n\nAllah's names Al-'Afuw (The Pardoner) and Al-Ghafoor (The Forgiving) are emphasized. He pardons completely (erasing sins as if they never happened) and forgives (covering faults with mercy). Those who were genuinely unable to emigrate can trust in Allah's comprehensive mercy.",
  "100": "'Muraghaman' means places of refuge where one can escape the enemy and live with dignity. 'Sa'ah' means spaciousness, abundance, and provision. Allah promises that those who leave their homes for His sake will find alternatives. The earth is not limited to one location. Those who sacrifice one home for Allah's sake will find others. This was proven by the early Muslims who emigrated to Abyssinia and Madinah—they found safety, community, and provision.\n\nThis addresses those who begin the journey of emigration but die before completing it. Even if they never reach their destination, their reward is guaranteed with Allah. The intention combined with the action of leaving is enough. The Prophet (peace be upon him) mentioned a man from Banu Layth who left to emigrate but died on the way—his reward was complete. This shows that Allah rewards sincere intention backed by genuine effort, even if the goal is not achieved.\n\nAny shortcomings in the emigration—perhaps imperfect intention, regrets, or difficulties—are covered by Allah's forgiveness and mercy.",
  "101": "This establishes the concession of 'qasr'—shortening the four-unit prayers (Dhuhr, Asr, Isha) to two units while traveling. Islam recognizes the difficulty of travel and provides ease. The Prophet (peace be upon him) practiced this throughout his life while traveling. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked the Prophet about this concession, and he said: 'It is a charity from Allah to you, so accept His charity.'\n\nThe verse mentions fear of enemy attack as a context, but the Prophet's practice showed that shortening applies to all travel, not just dangerous journeys. The mention of fear here highlights the additional reason during times of conflict.\n\nThis reminder contextualizes why caution is needed. The enmity of disbelievers toward believers is clear ('mubeen')—open and obvious. This justifies both the military alertness and the prayer concessions during hostile conditions.",
  "102": "This describes 'Salat al-Khawf'—the prayer of fear, performed during active military engagement. The army divides into two groups. One group prays with the imam while carrying weapons.\n\nAfter the first group completes their portion, they move to guard positions while still armed, protecting the congregation.\n\nThe second group then comes to pray, also carrying their weapons and remaining alert. This ensures continuous protection while still fulfilling the obligation of congregational prayer. Prayer is never abandoned, even in battle.\n\nThe enemy hopes Muslims will be distracted during prayer, creating an opportunity for attack. This verse warns against that vulnerability while showing that Islam doesn't require believers to be defenseless during worship.\n\nConcessions exist even within concessions. If carrying weapons is impractical due to weather or sickness, there is no sin in setting them aside—but mental alertness should remain.\n\nThis reassurance reminds believers that regardless of the enemy's plots, Allah has prepared their ultimate defeat and humiliation.",
  "103": "Even after the formal prayer ends, remembrance of Allah should continue in all positions—while marching, resting, or wounded. Dhikr (remembrance) is not limited to any posture and should fill the believer's moments, especially during dangerous times.\n\nWhen the danger passes and safety returns, the full prayers must be performed properly without shortcuts. The concessions during danger do not become permanent habits; they are temporary allowances.\n\nPrayer is 'kitaban mawqutan'—a prescribed obligation at fixed times. It cannot be abandoned, even in war. The times are set by Allah, and believers must fulfill this duty whether in comfort or difficulty. This verse establishes that prayer times are fixed and obligatory—one of the most important principles of Islamic worship.",
  "104": "'Wahn' means weakness, slackness, or losing resolve. Believers should not become weak or give up when pursuing a just cause against enemies. Persistence is required even when tired or suffering setbacks.\n\nThis puts hardship in perspective. Whatever pain, exhaustion, or losses the believers experience, the enemy experiences the same. Battle is hard for both sides. Neither has a monopoly on suffering. So why should believers be the ones to give up?\n\nHere is the crucial difference: believers have hope in Allah's reward—victory in this life, forgiveness of sins, martyrdom leading to Paradise, and eternal reward. The disbelievers have none of this. They suffer the same pain but with no divine hope, no eternal reward, no spiritual purpose. This difference should give believers the edge in perseverance.\n\nAllah knows the full picture—the struggles, the sacrifices, the hidden intentions, and the ultimate outcomes. His wisdom guides events toward the best conclusion. Trust His plan and persist.",
  "105": "The Quran was revealed with absolute truth—no falsehood in its content, its guidance, or its judgments. It is the criterion by which all matters are decided.\n\nThe Prophet's role includes being a judge among people, and his judgments must be based on divine guidance, not personal opinion or external pressure. 'What Allah has shown you' refers to the Quran, the Sunnah, and divine inspiration that guides the Prophet's decisions.\n\nThis verse was revealed regarding a case where a man from the Ansar stole a suit of armor and hid it with a Jewish neighbor, then blamed the Jew. Some people pressured the Prophet to defend the thief because he was Muslim. Allah warns against advocating for those who are guilty simply because of tribal, religious, or personal connections. Justice must be blind to such considerations. A 'khaseem' is someone who argues on behalf of another—the Prophet is told never to argue in defense of traitors or the deceitful.",
  "106": "Even the Prophet, who is protected from sin, is commanded to seek forgiveness. This teaches humility and constant turning to Allah. For ordinary believers, seeking forgiveness is even more essential. Some scholars say this may refer to seeking forgiveness for having initially considered the case favorably before the truth was revealed.\n\nAllah's forgiveness is always available for those who sincerely seek it. His mercy encompasses all who turn to Him in repentance.",
  "107": "Those who deceive and sin are ultimately harming themselves most. 'Yakhtanoon anfusahum' means they betray their own souls. By defending such people, one becomes complicit in their self-destruction.\n\n'Khawwan' (extremely treacherous) and 'atheem' (deeply sinful) are intensive forms, describing habitual, serious wrongdoing. Allah does not love such people, which is a severe condemnation. Being beloved to Allah is the greatest achievement; being disliked by Him is the greatest loss.",
  "108": "The deceitful are concerned about their reputation among humans but forget that Allah sees everything. They may successfully conceal their plots from people, but nothing is hidden from Allah. This is a fundamental problem—fearing people's judgment more than Allah's.\n\n'Yubayyitoon' means they spend the night planning and scheming evil. In the darkness, they think no one sees them, but Allah is with them, witnessing every whisper, every plot, every intention. Their nighttime conspiracies are fully known to Him.\n\n'Muheetan' means Allah's knowledge surrounds and encompasses everything they do. Nothing escapes Him—not the act, not the intention, not the consequences.",
  "109": "This is a direct address to those who defended the guilty party. You may argue on their behalf now, in this temporary world, using your influence or rhetoric.\n\nOn the Day of Judgment, when they stand before Allah with their crimes exposed, who will dare to defend them then? No lawyer, no influential friend, no tribal leader will step forward to argue their case before the Lord of the Worlds.\n\nWho will be their 'wakeel'—their representative, their defender? No one will take on that role against Allah. This rhetorical question exposes the futility of defending the guilty now when they will stand alone then.",
  "110": "'Su'' (evil) may refer to sins that harm others, while 'wronging oneself' may refer to sins that primarily harm the sinner—though all sins ultimately wrong oneself. Both categories are covered.\n\nThis is a beautiful promise. No matter what sin you have committed, if you sincerely turn to Allah in repentance, you will find Him ready to forgive. The verse guarantees it—'yajid' (will find). This keeps the door of hope open for all sinners. The thief mentioned in the story could have repented rather than adding to his sin by blaming an innocent person.",
  "111": "Sin may harm others temporarily, but ultimately, the sinner bears the true burden. The consequences of sin—guilt, spiritual darkness, punishment in the Hereafter—fall upon the one who commits it. You cannot truly harm Allah by disobeying Him; you only harm yourself.\n\nAllah knows every sin committed and, in His wisdom, assigns consequences accordingly. Nothing escapes His knowledge, and His judgment is always wise and just.",
  "112": "This directly addresses what the thief did—committing theft then falsely accusing an innocent man. This compounding of sin upon sin is extremely serious.\n\nNow they carry two burdens: (1) 'buhtan'—slander, false accusation, which is itself a major sin, and (2) 'ithm mubeen'—a clear, obvious sin that cannot be excused. The original sin was bad enough; adding false accusation makes it far worse. This teaches that covering one sin with another only multiplies guilt.",
  "113": "Allah protected the Prophet from being misled by the false case presented to him. Without divine intervention, the plotters might have succeeded in making the Prophet rule unjustly, which would have harmed his reputation and mission.\n\nTheir plot backfired. By attempting to deceive the Prophet, they only increased their own sin and exposed their own treachery. The deceiver ultimately deceives only themselves.\n\nAllah guarantees the Prophet's protection. No plot against him can succeed in causing real harm to him or his mission. This is reassurance for the Prophet and a warning to his enemies.\n\nAllah blessed the Prophet with (1) the Quran (the Book), (2) wisdom (hikmah—the Sunnah, understanding, and insight), and (3) knowledge he never had before. The Prophet was unlettered before prophethood; all his knowledge came from divine teaching.\n\nThe sum of all these blessings—protection, revelation, wisdom, knowledge—constitutes an immense favor from Allah. The Prophet should recognize this and remain grateful, and the enemies should recognize they are fighting against Allah's chosen messenger.",
  "114": "'Najwa' refers to private conversations, whispered discussions, confidential meetings. Most such talk is useless or harmful—gossip, plotting, backbiting, or empty chatter. Allah declares there is no 'khayr' (good) in most of it.\n\nPrivate conversations that promote giving, whether encouraging oneself or others to give charity, are good and beneficial.\n\n'Ma'roof' encompasses all good deeds—helping others, doing what is right, promoting virtue. Conversations that encourage good behavior are valuable.\n\nOne of the most meritorious acts is bringing peace between people who are in conflict. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Shall I not inform you of something better than fasting, prayer, and charity? It is reconciling people.' Making peace between spouses, family members, friends, or community members is highly rewarded.\n\nThe key condition is intention—'ibtighaa mardaatillah' (seeking Allah's pleasure). If these good deeds are done for Allah's sake, not for reputation or personal gain, the reward is 'great' ('atheem')—a term indicating tremendous recompense.",
  "115": "'Yushaqiq' means to oppose, resist, or create division from the Messenger. This is not about genuine ignorance but willful defiance after the truth is clearly understood. Such people know the right path but deliberately turn away.\n\nThis phrase is significant for Islamic jurisprudence. It establishes that following the consensus (ijma') of the believers is obligatory. Separating from the community of believers and taking one's own way is dangerous. The 'way of the believers' is the way of the Prophet's companions and those who follow them sincerely.\n\nAllah gives them what they wanted—their own misguided path. This is a form of punishment: being left to follow one's misguidance without divine guidance or protection.\n\nThe final destination of such people is Hellfire. After letting them wander in their chosen misguidance in this life, Allah will cast them into Hell forever. 'What an evil end' emphasizes the horror of this fate.",
  "116": "Shirk—directing any worship to other than Allah—is the only sin that Allah will not forgive if one dies upon it without repentance. This is repeated in the Quran to emphasize its severity. However, if a person repents from shirk before death, Allah can forgive even this.\n\nEvery other sin—no matter how severe—is subject to Allah's forgiveness. He may forgive through repentance, through good deeds, through hardships that expiate sins, or simply through His mercy. This gives hope to all sinners except those who die as polytheists.\n\n'Dalala ba'eeda' (far astray) describes someone who has wandered so far from the path that returning becomes extremely difficult. Shirk takes one far from truth, far from Allah, far from salvation.",
  "117": "The pagans of Arabia worshipped female deities like Al-Lat, Al-Uzza, and Manat, considering them daughters of Allah (which Allah refutes). They also gave feminine names to angels and worshipped them. Despite their pride in masculinity, they worshipped what they considered lesser beings.\n\nBehind all idol worship is Satan. Whatever names or forms the idols take, worshipping them is really worshipping Satan, who set up this system of polytheism. 'Mareed' describes Satan as rebellious, obstinate, and utterly defiant of Allah.",
  "118": "Satan is permanently expelled from Allah's mercy. This curse is his eternal fate.\n\nSatan declared his intention to claim a portion of humanity. 'Naseeban mafroodan' suggests a fixed, determined portion—he is committed to misleading as many as possible. This is his mission and his satisfaction—corrupting Allah's creation.",
  "119": "Satan's first tactic is leading people away from truth—making falsehood appear attractive and truth appear burdensome.\n\n'Umanniyannuhum'—he fills them with false hopes: 'You can repent later,' 'Hell is not real,' 'Allah will surely forgive,' 'Enjoy now and worry later.' These empty promises lead people to procrastinate repentance.\n\nThis refers to pagan practices of marking animals for idols by cutting their ears. Satan commands religious innovations and superstitious practices.\n\nThis encompasses changing what Allah has created—whether through prohibited body modifications, genetic tampering, or corrupting the natural disposition (fitrah) that Allah placed in humans. Satan wants humans to deform themselves and their nature.\n\nChoosing Satan as one's 'wali' (protector, ally) instead of Allah results in 'khusran mubeen'—a clear, obvious, devastating loss. Satan promises but never delivers; Allah promises and always fulfills.",
  "120": "This summarizes Satan's methodology. He promises pleasure, success, and delayed consequences, but none of his promises are real.\n\n'Ghuroor' means deception, delusion, false appearances. Everything Satan offers is a mirage—beautiful from afar but nothing when you arrive. His promises evaporate, leaving only regret.",
  "121": "Those who follow Satan will end up in Hellfire—their permanent dwelling ('ma'wa').\n\n'Mahees' means a place of escape or refuge. They will search for a way out but find none. Hell is inescapable for those condemned to it.",
  "122": "In contrast to Satan's followers, the believers who combine faith with righteous action will enter Paradise—gardens of eternal beauty with rivers flowing beneath them.\n\n'Khalideen feeha abada' emphasizes permanence—not just a long time, but eternal, forever. The joy never ends.\n\nUnlike Satan's false promises, Allah's promise is 'haqqa'—true, real, and certain. When Allah promises Paradise, it is guaranteed.\n\nThis rhetorical question establishes Allah as the ultimate source of truth. No one's word is more reliable. If you must trust someone's promise, trust Allah's.",
  "123": "Salvation is not achieved through wishful thinking. Muslims cannot say 'We are guaranteed Paradise because we are Muslim,' just as Jews and Christians cannot claim automatic salvation. Empty claims without righteous action mean nothing.\n\nThis is the universal law: you reap what you sow. Sin has consequences regardless of your religious identity. No group is exempt from accountability.\n\nWhen facing the consequences of sin, no one can protect or help except Allah. No tribal connection, no religious label, no wealth or influence will help.",
  "124": "The formula for Paradise is clear: faith plus good deeds. Both are required, and both are available to men and women equally. Gender does not determine one's potential for reward.\n\nThe 'naqeer' is the tiny speck in the groove of a date stone—virtually worthless. Not even this much will be unjustly taken from their rewards. Allah's justice is perfect.",
  "125": "'Aslama wajhahu' means to surrender one's face—one's entire self, direction, and being—to Allah. Complete submission is the essence of Islam.\n\n'Muhsin' describes one who does things excellently, with perfection and sincerity. It's not just doing good but doing it beautifully for Allah's sake.\n\n'Millat Ibrahim' is the pure monotheistic way of Abraham, free from polytheism and deviation. 'Haneef' means one who turns away from all falsehood toward pure truth. This is the path all prophets followed.\n\n'Khaleel' is a level of friendship beyond ordinary friendship—an intimate, exclusive relationship. Abraham was given this unique honor. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: 'If I were to take a khaleel from this ummah, I would have taken Abu Bakr, but your companion [referring to himself] is the khaleel of Allah.' This shows Abraham's exceptional status.",
  "126": "Allah is the owner of everything. Nothing exists outside His dominion. This ownership means He has full authority over all creation.\n\n'Muheetan' means Allah's knowledge encompasses and surrounds everything. Nothing is outside His awareness—every thought, every action, every hidden matter.",
  "127": "People asked the Prophet about various rulings concerning women. Allah answers directly, emphasizing that these rulings come from Him, not from cultural preferences.\n\nThis refers back to verse 4:3. Some guardians wanted to marry orphan women under their care to control their wealth, but without giving them fair dowries. They wanted the benefit of the marriage without fulfilling the woman's rights.\n\nAllah also instructs regarding weak, vulnerable children who cannot defend themselves. Their rights must be protected.\n\n'Qist' means justice and fairness. Standing up for orphans' rights is a repeated theme because orphans are easily exploited.\n\nEvery act of kindness toward women, orphans, and the vulnerable is recorded by Allah. He knows and will reward accordingly.",
  "128": "When a wife notices her husband becoming distant or neglectful—perhaps he wants to divorce her or marry another—they can negotiate a settlement. She might agree to forgo some of her rights (like some nights or financial support) to preserve the marriage. This happened with Sawdah (may Allah be pleased with her), who gave her night to Aisha to remain married to the Prophet.\n\n'Sulh is khair'—reconciliation and settlement is better than divorce. Islam encourages saving marriages when possible.\n\n'Shuhh' means extreme stinginess or selfishness. Human nature inclines toward protecting self-interest, which can make compromise difficult. But marriage requires sacrifice and compromise from both sides.\n\nIf spouses show grace, forgiveness, and taqwa, Allah sees and rewards it. Good conduct in marriage, even when difficult, does not go unnoticed.",
  "129": "Complete emotional equality between wives is impossible. The heart's inclinations cannot be perfectly controlled. A man might love one wife more than another, and this is beyond his control.\n\nWhile emotional equality is impossible, material fairness is required. Don't neglect one wife so completely that she is 'mu'allaqah'—suspended, neither properly married nor divorced. Give each wife her rights of time, support, and dignity even if emotional attachment varies.\n\nIf you try your best to be fair despite natural emotional limitations, Allah will forgive shortcomings and show mercy.",
  "130": "If reconciliation fails and divorce becomes necessary, both parties should trust that Allah will provide for them. Divorce is not the end—Allah's provision is vast. Each can find happiness and sustenance separately.\n\nAllah's bounty ('sa'ah') is limitless, and His wisdom determines what is best for each person. Sometimes separation, though painful, is the wisest outcome.",
  "131": "Allah's ownership is total and absolute. This is repeated to emphasize His sovereignty.\n\nTaqwa (mindfulness of Allah) was commanded to all previous nations and to Muslims. It is the universal teaching of all divine revelations.\n\nYour disobedience does not harm Allah in the least. He owns everything and needs nothing from you. Your obedience benefits you; your disobedience harms you.\n\n'Ghaniyy' means Allah needs nothing from anyone. 'Hameed' means He is worthy of all praise regardless of whether creation praises Him. His perfection is independent of human acknowledgment.",
  "132": "The repetition drives home this fundamental truth.\n\nAllah is the perfect 'wakeel'—manager and disposer of all affairs. Those who trust Him need no other support.",
  "133": "This is a reminder of human dispensability. Allah does not need us. If all humans were to disobey, He could simply remove them and create a new, obedient creation. This should inspire humility and gratitude for the opportunity to worship and be rewarded.\n\nThis is not an empty threat—Allah has full power to execute this if He willed. His ability is unlimited.",
  "134": "Some people only work for worldly gain—wealth, fame, power. But they should know that Allah controls both worldly and eternal rewards. Why settle for just worldly benefit when you could have both? Why not ask Allah, who owns everything, for both worlds?\n\nAllah hears all requests and sees all intentions. He knows who is working for this world alone and who seeks both worlds.",
  "135": "'Qawwameen bil-qist' means to be upholders, establishers, and maintainers of justice—not occasionally, but consistently. Being a witness 'for Allah' means your testimony aims to please Allah, not to benefit yourself or others unjustly.\n\nTrue justice requires testifying truthfully even when it harms your own interests, even when it goes against your beloved parents, even when it exposes your closest relatives. This is extremely difficult but essential for a just society.\n\nDon't let someone's wealth intimidate you or someone's poverty invoke false sympathy. Don't favor the rich out of hope for their favor, or the poor out of misplaced compassion. Allah knows what is best for both and will take care of them. Your job is to speak the truth.\n\n'Hawa' (desires) should never influence testimony or judgment. Personal likes, dislikes, benefits, or fears must not distort justice.\n\n'Talwoo' means to distort or twist testimony. 'Tu'ridoo' means to refuse to testify when you have knowledge. Both are sinful. Allah is 'khabeer'—fully aware of every detail. You cannot hide the truth from Him.",
  "136": "Even those who already believe are called to believe—meaning to increase, renew, and perfect their faith. Faith is not static; it should grow continuously.\n\nFaith in Muhammad (peace be upon him) as Allah's final messenger is essential. This includes following his guidance and accepting his authority.\n\nThe Quran—the final, preserved revelation that supersedes previous scriptures.\n\nThe original Torah, Gospel, Psalms, and other divine scriptures—believing that they were originally from Allah, though later altered by human hands.\n\nThese are the pillars of faith. Denying any one of them takes a person 'far astray' (dalalan ba'eeda)—not just slightly off course but completely lost. These beliefs are fundamental and interconnected; rejecting one undermines all.",
  "137": "This describes people who treat faith like a revolving door—entering and exiting as convenient. They embrace Islam when it benefits them, leave when it becomes difficult, return when circumstances change, then abandon it again. This pattern shows they never had sincere faith.\n\nEach cycle of apostasy deepens their disbelief. Rather than learning from their experiences, they become more entrenched in rejection. Their hearts become harder with each return to disbelief.\n\nFor such persistent waverers who die in disbelief, there is no forgiveness and no guidance. They have played games with their faith so many times that Allah seals their hearts. This is not because Allah cannot forgive, but because they have demonstrated complete insincerity and will not genuinely repent.",
  "138": "This is bitter sarcasm. 'Good news' (bashara) is typically used for glad tidings, but here it sarcastically announces punishment. The hypocrites who thought they were clever, playing both sides, will receive painful torment. Their 'reward' for their deception is suffering.",
  "139": "The hypocrites align themselves with disbelievers against the Muslim community. They share secrets with enemies, support them financially or politically, and prefer their company over that of sincere believers.\n\nThe rhetorical question exposes their motivation—they think allying with powerful disbelievers will bring them honor, status, or security. They calculate that the disbelievers might win, so they hedge their bets.\n\nTheir calculation is fundamentally flawed. True 'izzah' (honor, power, dignity) belongs entirely to Allah. He grants it to whom He wills. Seeking honor from those who reject Allah is seeking it from an empty source. Only through Allah can genuine honor be achieved.",
  "140": "This refers to a previous revelation in Surah Al-An'am (6:68). When people mock, deny, or make fun of Allah's verses, the Quran, Islam, or the Prophet, believers must respond appropriately.\n\nThe command is clear: leave such gatherings. Don't be a passive audience to blasphemy or mockery. You may return only if they change the subject to something permissible. This applies to physical gatherings, online forums, social media discussions, and any setting where Islam is being ridiculed.\n\nSilent approval makes you a participant. By staying and listening without objection or leaving, you become complicit in their sin. Your presence legitimizes their mockery.\n\nThose who mock and those who silently approve will share the same destination. The hypocrites who sat with mockers, enjoying the 'humor' while claiming to be Muslim, will join the disbelievers in Hell.",
  "141": "'Yatarabbasoon' means they watch from the sidelines, waiting to see which side wins before committing. They are strategic opportunists who never take a principled stand.\n\nWhen Muslims win, hypocrites rush to claim association. 'We were with you all along! Count us among the victorious!' They want a share of the spoils without having shared the risk.\n\nWhen disbelievers gain ground, hypocrites rush to their side, claiming credit for helping them. 'We had inside information on the Muslims but didn't use it fully against you. We protected you.' They try to collect rewards from both sides.\n\nThis double-dealing will be exposed and judged. On that Day, their true allegiance will be revealed.\n\nUltimately, true believers will prevail. Allah promises that disbelievers will never have ultimate authority or permanent victory over sincere believers—whether in this world or the next.",
  "142": "The hypocrites think their pretense fools Allah, but He knows their reality completely. 'He outwits them' means He lets them continue in their delusion, gives them rope to hang themselves, and prepares their punishment while they think they're succeeding.\n\n'Kusala' means lazily, reluctantly, with heaviness. They drag themselves to prayer with no enthusiasm. Their bodies pray while their hearts are elsewhere. This describes the outer form without inner spirit.\n\n'Riya'' (showing off) is their motivation. They pray not for Allah but for social acceptance, to maintain their cover as Muslims. If no one was watching, they wouldn't pray at all.\n\nEven during prayer, their minds wander everywhere except to Allah. They recite without understanding, bow without humility, and prostrate without submission. Their remembrance of Allah is minimal and insincere.",
  "143": "'Mudhabdhabeen' describes someone swaying, wavering, unstable—like something blown by wind from side to side. They cannot commit to either side. Not truly Muslim, not openly kafir. This is the worst position—at least the open disbeliever has clarity.\n\nWhen Allah allows someone to go astray due to their persistent insincerity, no one can guide them. The Prophet himself cannot guide those whom Allah has left in misguidance. Guidance is Allah's gift, and hypocrisy forfeits it.",
  "144": "This is a direct command to the Muslim community. 'Awliya'' in this context means close allies, confidants, protectors—those with whom you share secrets and upon whom you rely against your own community. Normal interactions, business dealings, and neighborly relations are not prohibited, but deep alliance that compromises Muslim interests is forbidden.\n\nBy allying with those who oppose Islam against fellow Muslims, you provide clear evidence for your own punishment. On the Day of Judgment, such behavior will stand as proof of hypocrisy or weak faith.",
  "145": "'Darak al-asfal' is the lowest, deepest level of Hell—worse than the level of open disbelievers. Hypocrites are punished more severely because their crime is worse: they pretended to believe while secretly rejecting, causing harm from within the Muslim community.\n\nIn the deepest Hell, no one will come to their aid. No intercessor, no rescuer, no relief. Their isolation is complete.",
  "146": "The door of repentance remains open even for hypocrites. Sincere tawbah (repentance) can save them from the lowest Hell.\n\n'Aslahu' means to correct, reform, and fix what was broken. Repentance must be followed by changing behavior and repairing damage done.\n\n'I'tasamu billah'—to grip tightly to Allah, seeking refuge in Him alone, relying on Him completely. This is the opposite of seeking protection from disbelievers.\n\n'Akhlasu deenahum lillah'—making their religion purely for Allah, removing all hypocrisy, showing off, and mixed intentions. Sincerity (ikhlas) is the cure for hypocrisy.\n\nThose who fulfill these conditions are no longer hypocrites—they join the ranks of true believers.\n\nThe reward for true faith is immense—not just escape from Hell, but Paradise and Allah's pleasure.",
  "147": "This rhetorical question reveals Allah's nature. He doesn't punish for the sake of punishment. If you believe and are grateful, what reason would Allah have to punish you? His punishment is a consequence of disbelief and ingratitude, not arbitrary cruelty.\n\n'Shakir' means Allah appreciates and rewards even small acts of gratitude and faith. 'Aleem' means He knows exactly who is sincere. He won't overlook genuine faith or miss hidden hypocrisy.",
  "148": "Generally, Islam discourages speaking evil—backbiting, slander, public complaints, cursing, and spreading negativity. Allah does not love such speech.\n\nThe exception is for victims of injustice. They may speak out about the wrong done to them—to seek help, to warn others, or to seek justice. A person who has been cheated may name the cheater. An abuse victim may speak about the abuser. This is not forbidden negative speech but legitimate recourse.\n\nAllah hears both the wrongdoer and the victim. He knows who truly was wronged and who is making false claims. This reminder ensures the exception isn't abused.",
  "149": "Good deeds can be done publicly (to encourage others) or privately (for greater sincerity). Both are rewarded based on intention.\n\nWhile the victim has the right to speak out, pardoning is even better. Forgiving those who wronged you, letting go of grievances—this is a higher path.\n\n'Afuww' (Ever-Pardoning) shows Allah loves to pardon, so He loves when His servants pardon others. 'Qadeer' (Most Capable) indicates that Allah has full power to punish but chooses to pardon—teaching us that true strength is in forgiving when you have the power to retaliate.",
  "150": "This addresses those who claim to believe in God but reject His messengers, or accept some messengers while rejecting others.\n\nThey try to separate belief in God from belief in His messengers—accepting God but rejecting prophets, or accepting prophets selectively.\n\nThis was the practice of Jews (accepting Moses but rejecting Jesus and Muhammad) and Christians (accepting Jesus but rejecting Muhammad). Selective belief in prophets is rejection of all, because all prophets came with the same essential message from the same God.\n\nThey want a middle path—partial faith that satisfies their preferences while rejecting what they dislike. Islam doesn't allow such pick-and-choose religion.",
  "151": "'Kafireen haqqan'—disbelievers in reality, in truth. Despite their claims of partial belief, they are truly disbelievers because rejecting one messenger means rejecting the One who sent all messengers.\n\nTheir punishment will include humiliation—fitting for those who arrogantly picked and chose among Allah's messengers.",
  "152": "True believers accept all prophets without discrimination. They believe in Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, Muhammad, and all prophets mentioned or unmentioned in the Quran.\n\nComplete faith earns complete reward. Those who accept the whole message will receive their full recompense.\n\nAllah forgives past ignorance when one comes to true faith and shows mercy to those who submit completely.",
  "153": "Some Jews asked Prophet Muhammad to bring down a physical book from the sky as proof. This was a demand for miracles on their terms, not sincere inquiry.\n\nTheir ancestors made an even more outrageous demand of Moses—to see Allah directly. This shows a pattern of making impossible demands to avoid believing.\n\nTheir arrogant demand brought divine punishment—they were struck by lightning for their transgression.\n\nDespite witnessing miracles, they worshipped a golden calf when Moses was away. This shows how quickly they abandoned faith despite overwhelming evidence.\n\nAllah's mercy is vast. Despite their terrible sin, when they repented, He forgave them and continued to support Moses with clear authority and miracles.",
  "154": "Mount Sinai was raised above them as a powerful sign when they hesitated to accept the covenant. This dramatic intervention compelled them to take their commitment seriously.\n\nWhen entering Jerusalem, they were commanded to enter humbly, bowing in gratitude. Instead, some entered mockingly.\n\nObserving the Sabbath was a strict commandment. They were forbidden from working or fishing on Saturday.\n\nThe covenant was solemn and serious ('ghaleetha' - firm, heavy, binding). Breaking it would have severe consequences.",
  "155": "Their punishment came because they violated the solemn promises they made to Allah.\n\nThey denied the miracles and revelations sent to them despite clear evidence.\n\nThey killed prophets sent to guide them—a crime of the highest magnitude.\n\nThey claimed their hearts were wrapped up, unable to receive new guidance—using this as an excuse for rejection.\n\nTheir claim was false—their hearts weren't naturally unreceptive; rather, Allah sealed them as punishment for persistent disbelief.\n\nOnly a small minority among them accepted truth—like Abdullah ibn Salam and other righteous individuals who embraced Islam.",
  "156": "Some Jews accused Mary (Maryam) of fornication—a terrible slander against one of the most righteous women in history. This 'buhtan atheem' (monstrous slander) added to their sins.",
  "157": "Some boasted about killing Jesus, though ironically they called him 'Messenger of Allah' (perhaps sarcastically, or this is the Quran's description). They took pride in what they thought was his execution.\n\nThis is the Islamic position on the crucifixion. Jesus was not killed or crucified—it was made to appear that way. 'Shubbiha lahum' means it was made to seem so to them. Many scholars say another person was made to look like Jesus and was crucified in his place.\n\nThose who claim certainty about the crucifixion are actually uncertain. There has been historical debate even among Christians about details.\n\nTheir belief is based on assumption (dhann), not certain knowledge. They followed what they thought happened, not verified truth.\n\nThis emphatic repetition reinforces the Islamic position—Jesus was definitely not killed by them.",
  "158": "Instead of being killed, Jesus was raised by Allah to the heavens. He is alive with Allah, saved from his enemies. This is the Islamic belief about Jesus's end on earth—not death by crucifixion but elevation to Allah.\n\nAllah had the power to save His messenger and the wisdom to do so in a way that tested people's faith and exposed the truth.",
  "159": "This verse has two main interpretations: (1) Before each individual from the People of the Book dies, they will believe in Jesus truthfully—perhaps at the moment of death when reality becomes clear. (2) Before Jesus's death—meaning when Jesus returns before the Day of Judgment, all People of the Book will believe in him as a prophet, not as God or son of God.\n\nJesus will testify against those who rejected him and those who made false claims about him—whether denying his prophethood or exaggerating him to divinity.",
  "160": "As punishment for their sins, Allah prohibited certain foods that were originally permissible—such as fat of cattle and sheep, and some other animals. These restrictions were punitive, not arbitrary.\n\nThey not only strayed themselves but actively prevented others from following truth. This compounded their sin.",
  "161": "Riba (interest/usury) was forbidden in their scripture, yet they practiced it extensively. This economic exploitation was a major sin.\n\nBeyond interest, they used various means to take others' money unfairly—fraud, manipulation, dishonest dealings.\n\nNot all Jews are condemned—only 'the disbelievers among them.' Those who rejected truth and practiced these sins face painful punishment.",
  "162": "'Rasikhoon fil-'ilm'—those with deep, firm knowledge. Not superficial learners but scholars who understood the truth of their scriptures.\n\nThey accept both the Quran and previous revelations, recognizing the continuity of Allah's message.\n\nPrayer is highlighted as a distinguishing practice of the sincere.\n\nThey fulfill financial obligations (zakat) and hold correct beliefs about Allah and the Hereafter.\n\nThe righteous among the People of the Book who accept Islam will receive tremendous reward—equal to any other believer.",
  "163": "Muhammad's prophethood follows the same pattern as all previous prophets. He received revelation just as they did—he is not an innovation but a continuation.\n\nThe Abrahamic line of prophets all received divine revelation.\n\nVarious prophets from different times and contexts are named—showing the breadth of Allah's guidance throughout history.\n\nDavid received the Zabur (Psalms)—confirming that multiple scriptures were revealed, not just the Torah.",
  "164": "The Quran mentions some prophets by name and story, but many others are not mentioned. Tradition suggests there were 124,000 prophets throughout history. We don't know all their names.\n\nMoses had the unique honor of direct speech with Allah—hence his title 'Kaleemullah' (the one Allah spoke to). This was a special distinction.",
  "165": "Every prophet came with both glad tidings for the obedient and warnings for the disobedient. This dual message is consistent throughout.\n\nThe purpose of sending so many messengers is to remove any excuse on the Day of Judgment. No one can say, 'We didn't know' or 'No one warned us.' Allah sent guidance to every nation.\n\nAllah's power ensured the message reached humanity, and His wisdom determined when and how to send each messenger.",
  "166": "Even if humans reject, Allah Himself testifies to the truth of the Quran. This divine testimony outweighs any human denial.\n\nThe Quran contains Allah's knowledge—perfect, complete, and without error. It wasn't the Prophet's invention but divine revelation.\n\nThe angels, including Jibril who delivered the revelation, also testify to its truth.\n\nAllah's testimony alone is enough. Even without any human or angelic witness, Allah's witness is complete and sufficient proof.",
  "167": "Those who not only disbelieve themselves but actively prevent others from believing have gone extremely far astray. They combine personal rejection with the crime of misleading others.",
  "168": "Those who combine disbelief with persistent wrongdoing lose access to forgiveness and guidance. Their fate is sealed if they die in that state.",
  "169": "The only path left for such people leads to Hell—eternal residence with no escape.\n\nExecuting this punishment is effortless for Allah. His power is unlimited. Those who thought they could escape or that Allah couldn't punish them are gravely mistaken.",
  "170": "This is a universal address to all human beings—not just Arabs, not just the people of that time, but all of humanity across all generations. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) came with absolute truth from the Lord of all worlds. This truth is not his own invention but divine revelation from Allah, your Creator and Sustainer.\n\nThe command to believe is followed by a reminder that faith benefits YOU, not Allah. Believing brings you guidance, peace, purpose in this life, and salvation in the Hereafter. Faith is in your own interest.\n\nYour disbelief doesn't harm Allah in the slightest. He owns everything—every star, every planet, every creature. He is completely independent and self-sufficient. Your rejection only harms yourself.\n\nAllah knows who believes and who rejects, and in His wisdom, He will deal with each accordingly. Nothing escapes His knowledge, and His decisions are always wise.",
  "171": "'Ghuluww' means to exceed proper bounds, to exaggerate, to go to extremes. This addresses Christians primarily, who elevated Jesus beyond his true status. Extremism in religion—whether exaggerating the status of prophets or saints, or adding practices not sanctioned by God—leads to deviation. The Jews went to one extreme by rejecting Jesus entirely; the Christians went to the other extreme by deifying him. Islam calls for the balanced middle path.\n\nAll statements about Allah must be true and verified. Claiming Allah has a son, or is part of a trinity, or has partners—these are false statements about Allah that must be avoided.\n\nThis clarifies Jesus's true status. He was a messenger—honored, noble, miraculous, but still a human messenger. 'Son of Mary' emphasizes his human birth and maternal lineage, implicitly denying any divine paternity.\n\nJesus was created through Allah's word 'Be!' (Kun). Allah's command was delivered to Mary, and Jesus came into existence. The 'word' refers to the creative command, not that Jesus himself is the eternal Word of God in a divine sense.\n\n'Rooh minhu'—a spirit from Him. This means Jesus was given life by Allah's command, just as Adam was. 'From Him' indicates origin of creation, not that Jesus is part of Allah's essence. Allah breathed life into Jesus as He did with Adam. This does not make Jesus divine any more than it makes Adam divine.\n\nTrue faith requires believing in Allah as One and in all His messengers, including Jesus—but as a messenger, not as God.\n\nThe doctrine of Trinity—Father, Son, and Holy Spirit as three persons in one God—is explicitly rejected. This concept developed after Jesus and contradicts pure monotheism.\n\n'Intahu'—cease, stop, desist! This is a strong command. Abandoning the Trinity doctrine is for their own benefit, not Allah's.\n\nTawheed—the absolute oneness of Allah—is the core message of all prophets. There is only one God, not three, not two, but ONE.\n\n'Subhanahu'—He is exalted, glorified, and far beyond such limitations. Having a child implies need, dependency, physicality—attributes that don't apply to Allah. He is the Creator, not a creature who reproduces.\n\nEverything is His creation and possession—including Jesus. The Owner doesn't become one of His possessions.\n\nAllah manages all affairs perfectly. He needs no son, no partner, no helper.",
  "172": "Jesus himself would never refuse to be called Allah's servant ('abd). Far from finding servitude to Allah degrading, Jesus embraced it. He never claimed divinity; he worshipped Allah. This verse uses Jesus's own humility to refute those who elevate him beyond his station.\n\nEven the highest-ranking angels—those closest to Allah like Jibril—consider themselves servants of Allah. If such exalted beings don't disdain servitude to Allah, why would anyone claim a human prophet is too noble to be a servant?\n\nRegardless of their arrogance in this life, all creatures will be gathered before Allah. Pride and arrogance won't save them then. They refused to bow willingly; they will stand before Him regardless.",
  "173": "Believers who back up their faith with righteous actions will receive their complete reward—nothing will be diminished or lost.\n\nBeyond full reward, Allah will add extra from His bounty. This 'fadl' (grace) is a bonus—reward beyond what was strictly earned. Allah's generosity exceeds what we deserve.\n\nThose who refused to worship Allah out of pride and arrogance will face severe punishment. Pride was Satan's downfall; it will be theirs too.\n\nWhen the punishment comes, no one will protect them or come to their aid. The false objects of worship—whether human, angelic, or imaginary—will be useless. Only Allah could have helped them, but they rejected Him.",
  "174": "'Burhan' means clear proof, decisive evidence that leaves no room for doubt. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), his message, and his miracles constitute this burhan. This proof comes from 'your Lord'—emphasizing Allah's relationship with all humanity as their Creator and Sustainer.\n\n'Noor mubin'—a clear, radiant, illuminating light. This refers to the Quran, which dispels the darkness of ignorance, superstition, and misguidance. It illuminates the path to Allah, showing clearly what is right and wrong, true and false.",
  "175": "'I'tasamu bihi'—to grip tightly, to cling firmly. This describes believers who not only believe but hold onto Allah for support, protection, and guidance. They don't let go despite difficulties.\n\nSuch believers will enter Allah's special mercy (rahmah) and His additional bounty (fadl). These are two layers of divine blessing—mercy that covers their sins and grace that elevates them further.\n\nThe ultimate destination is Allah Himself—His pleasure, His presence, His Paradise. The Straight Path (sirat al-mustaqeem) leads directly to Him. This guidance is continuous—He doesn't just show the path once; He guides them along it to completion.",
  "176": "This verse addresses 'kalalah'—a person who dies leaving neither children nor parents. Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) fell ill and asked the Prophet about his inheritance since he had no children or parents, only siblings. This verse was revealed clarifying the ruling. It deals specifically with full siblings (same father and mother), distinguishing from verse 4:12 which dealt with maternal half-siblings.\n\nWhen a man dies without children and has one full sister, she receives half of his estate. This is her fixed share (fard).\n\nIf the situation is reversed—a woman dies childless leaving only a brother—he inherits everything. As a male relative (asabah), he takes whatever remains after fixed shares, and if there are no other heirs, he takes all.\n\nIf the deceased has two or more full sisters but no brothers, the sisters together receive two-thirds. This parallels the rule for daughters.\n\nWhen there are both brothers and sisters, they share as 'asabah' (residuary heirs), with each male receiving twice the share of each female. This 2:1 ratio reflects the male's financial responsibilities in Islamic law.\n\nThese detailed inheritance laws prevent disputes, injustice, and confusion. Without clear divine guidance, people would go astray in distributing wealth, either through ignorance or favoritism.\n\nAllah's knowledge encompasses all family situations, all relationships, and all financial circumstances. His laws account for every scenario with perfect justice. Surah An-Nisa ends as it began—with inheritance laws, emphasizing the importance of financial justice in family matters and the comprehensive nature of Islamic guidance."
};
